[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Qxd5 and 3...Bg4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/39858176-3d3c-4c79-a05a-c58f68671545/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {11% of the time, Black replies with 1...d5, The Scandivanian Defense, and it was played in more than 400 million games. The main idea behind 1...d5 is to trade off the central pawns and get the queen out early so that Black can develop the queenside quickly, often choosing to castle long. We'll take the pawn.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen 72% of the time. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system, where we hope to develop with Be2, O-O, d4/c4 and so on. The Scandi has various setups for Black and general ideas to follow, but overall White's ideas are the same.} 3.Nf3 3...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight 22% of the time. We'll support and unpin the knight by developing our bishop to e2.} 4.Be2 4...Nc6 {In this setup, it's clear that Black is about to castle queenside, so there is no point in us going d4 to give them a target on the d-file. We'll just castle our king and go from there.} 5.O-O 5...O-O-O {Black castles their king. Let's ask the bishop a question now.} 6.h3 6...Bh5 {Black slides the bishop back to h5. We finally develop the queenside knight with tempo on Black's queen.} 7.Nc3 7...Qa5 {Black moves the queen over to a5. I had once recommended 8.a3 here, but 8.b4 is even stronger. The idea is to sacrifice the b-pawn and open up the b-file since Black has committed to the queenside.} 8.b4 8...Qxb4 {This move willingly puts the queen on the open file where it will be attacked. We are now enjoying b-file compensation.} 9.Rb1 9...Qd6 {The queen has to go to safety. Before we move our pieces further, it's principled to control the center.} 10.d4 10...e6 {Black pushes a pawn and tries to catch up on development. But we're too quick with the attack!} 11.Nb5 11...Qe7 {The queen moves away. We develop our bishop with a tempo. Now the c7-pawn is under fire.} 12.Bf4 12...Rd7 {The rook defends.} 13.Ne5 {And we're recapturing the pawn with a winning position. Black is uncoordinated and we're going to win some material, with an attack on the king. Everything is loose in the Black camp.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Qxd5 and 3...Bg4 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/39858176-3d3c-4c79-a05a-c58f68671545/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {11% of the time, Black replies with 1...d5, The Scandivanian Defense, and it was played in more than 400 million games. The main idea behind 1...d5 is to trade off the central pawns and get the queen out early so that Black can develop the queenside quickly, often choosing to castle long. We'll take the pawn.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen 72% of the time. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system, where we hope to develop with Be2, O-O, d4/c4 and so on. The Scandi has various setups for Black and general ideas to follow, but overall White's ideas are the same.} 3.Nf3 3...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight 22% of the time. We'll support and unpin the knight by developing our bishop to e2.} 4.Be2 4...Nc6 {In this setup, it's clear that Black is about to castle queenside, so there is no point in us going d4 to give them a target on the d-file. We'll just castle our king and go from there.} 5.O-O 5...O-O-O {Black castles their king. Let's ask the bishop a question now.} 6.h3 6...h5 {The move ...h5 is yet another fishing hook idea where Black hopes to open the h-file and create a strong attack. Our plan is to play d4, not to take the bishop and play Black's game. We want to first prepare it with c3.} 7.c3 7...e5 {Black plays e5 to control the center, which is one of their best approaches. When there's a fishing hook trap like this with a pawn on the 5th rank, Black will take longer to reach the h-file since the e5-pawn blocks the queen. Hence, we can capture.} 8.hxg4 8...hxg4 {Black recaptures and the h-file is opened. But we put yet another piece on the 5th rank, slowing down Black's counterplay. The g4-pawn is also under attack now.} 9.Ng5 9...f5 {Black defends g4. Not only is Qb3 a queen trade proposal, it's our pieces converging on f7.} 10.Qb3 10...Qd6 {Black avoids the trade, which allows us to jump to f7. This is a family fork.} 11.Nf7 11...Qf6 {The queen goes to f6. We take on h8.} 12.Nxh8 12...Nh6 {Black tries preventing the escape of the knight. But with d3 we attack the knight and pose even more problems.} 13.d3 13...Bd6 {Black wants to take the knight, and we can't really defend or move it. But we can use this time to solidify and slow the pawns and pieces marching forward. g2 is also a great square for our king since we control the light squares.} 14.g3 14...Rxh8 {The rook captures our knight. But we are solid.} 15.Kg2 15...f4 {Black pushes ...f4. The threat is ...f3.} 16.Qd1 {The good thing about being up material is that you can give some back and still have a winning position and more material. We are ready to face ...f3 here and sacrifice. White is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Qxd5 and 3...Bg4 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/39858176-3d3c-4c79-a05a-c58f68671545/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {11% of the time, Black replies with 1...d5, The Scandivanian Defense, and it was played in more than 400 million games. The main idea behind 1...d5 is to trade off the central pawns and get the queen out early so that Black can develop the queenside quickly, often choosing to castle long. We'll take the pawn.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen 72% of the time. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system, where we hope to develop with Be2, O-O, d4/c4 and so on. The Scandi has various setups for Black and general ideas to follow, but overall White's ideas are the same.} 3.Nf3 3...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight 22% of the time. We'll support and unpin the knight by developing our bishop to e2.} 4.Be2 4...Nc6 {In this setup, it's clear that Black is about to castle queenside, so there is no point in us going d4 to give them a target on the d-file. We'll just castle our king and go from there.} 5.O-O 5...O-O-O {Black castles their king. Let's ask the bishop a question now.} 6.h3 6...h5 {The move ...h5 is yet another fishing hook idea where Black hopes to open the h-file and create a strong attack. Our plan is to play d4, not to take the bishop and play Black's game. We want to first prepare it with c3.} 7.c3 7...Nf6 {Not placing any pawns in the center isn't ideal. We do put a pawn in the center, though. Black's position isn't bad yet, but it can become so if they don't play actively.} 8.d4 8...e6 {This passive continuation is the most common. I like Re1 because we improve our rook, but we also get access to f1. Our king won't get mated easily.} 9.Re1 9...Bd6 {Black may develop their bishop to d6. The knight has many options. The f1-square allows our pieces to pivot and defend our king.} 10.Nbd2 10...Qf5 {Black has a clear plan. But we have enough pieces to thwart this attack. We transport our knight to defend the king.} 11.Nf1 11...Bxh3 {Black takes, but they aren't winning, nor do they have a forced draw. We first attack the queen.} 12.Ne3 12...Qg6 {The queen pins our pawn. But we have a bishop that we can use to harass the queen. There are simply too many defenders.} 13.Bd3 13...Bf5 {This move seems normal but it's a mistake. We capture on f5.} 14.Nxf5 14...exf5 {Black recaptures.} 15.Nh4 {And once again as we see many times in this chapter, the queen is trapped. Well done!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Qxd5 and 3...Bg4 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/39858176-3d3c-4c79-a05a-c58f68671545/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {11% of the time, Black replies with 1...d5, The Scandivanian Defense, and it was played in more than 400 million games. The main idea behind 1...d5 is to trade off the central pawns and get the queen out early so that Black can develop the queenside quickly, often choosing to castle long. We'll take the pawn.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen 72% of the time. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system, where we hope to develop with Be2, O-O, d4/c4 and so on. The Scandi has various setups for Black and general ideas to follow, but overall White's ideas are the same.} 3.Nf3 3...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight 22% of the time. We'll support and unpin the knight by developing our bishop to e2.} 4.Be2 4...Nc6 {In this setup, it's clear that Black is about to castle queenside, so there is no point in us going d4 to give them a target on the d-file. We'll just castle our king and go from there.} 5.O-O 5...O-O-O {Black castles their king. Let's ask the bishop a question now.} 6.h3 6...h5 {The move ...h5 is yet another fishing hook idea where Black hopes to open the h-file and create a strong attack. Our plan is to play d4, not to take the bishop and play Black's game. We want to first prepare it with c3.} 7.c3 7...Nf6 {Not placing any pawns in the center isn't ideal. We do put a pawn in the center, though. Black's position isn't bad yet, but it can become so if they don't play actively.} 8.d4 8...e6 {This passive continuation is the most common. I like Re1 because we improve our rook, but we also get access to f1. Our king won't get mated easily.} 9.Re1 9...g5 {One of Black's potential plans is always a kingside attack. But we are unbothered: development and a solid position allow us to defend any incoming unsound attacks.} 10.Nbd2 10...Bxf3 {In order for the attack to progress Black has to take. But we recapture, with a strong bishop.} 11.Bxf3 11...Qd7 {The queen retreats to d7. At this point, I like simplifying. Sometimes, there's a clear way to end an attack and we have to take it. First we'll capture.} 12.Bxc6 12...Qxc6 {After we attract the queen to c6 we have a simplification tactic.} 13.Qf3 {We attack the queen and the knight. This represents a better endgame for us. Black's g5- and h5-pawns are weak.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Qxd5 and 3...Bg4 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/39858176-3d3c-4c79-a05a-c58f68671545/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {11% of the time, Black replies with 1...d5, The Scandivanian Defense, and it was played in more than 400 million games. The main idea behind 1...d5 is to trade off the central pawns and get the queen out early so that Black can develop the queenside quickly, often choosing to castle long. We'll take the pawn.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen 72% of the time. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system, where we hope to develop with Be2, O-O, d4/c4 and so on. The Scandi has various setups for Black and general ideas to follow, but overall White's ideas are the same.} 3.Nf3 3...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight 22% of the time. We'll support and unpin the knight by developing our bishop to e2.} 4.Be2 4...Nc6 {In this setup, it's clear that Black is about to castle queenside, so there is no point in us going d4 to give them a target on the d-file. We'll just castle our king and go from there.} 5.O-O 5...O-O-O {Black castles their king. Let's ask the bishop a question now.} 6.h3 6...Bxf3 {The capture on f3 is quite natural. We like to see it because we get the bishop pair. We recapture, with a powerful bishop aiming at the light squares towards the king.} 7.Bxf3 7...Qd7 {The queen retreats, keeping an eye on the c6-knight. We centralize the rook. Another natural improving move.} 8.Re1 8...e6 {Black's plan is easy in this equal position, as they want to develop the pieces to d6 and f6. We're going to develop our pieces as well, but we also want to make sure we have a strong center. The move c3 serves to prepare d4.} 9.c3 9...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to f6. And we get the strong d4-pawn in the center. The e5-square is completely under our control.} 10.d4 10...Bd6 {The bishop goes to d6, aiming at the kingside but most importantly that central e5-square.} 11.Nd2 {But we are going to attack the bishop to remove it from d6. If Black allows it, their position can become passive very quickly as you can see here. White is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Qxd5 and 3...Bg4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/39858176-3d3c-4c79-a05a-c58f68671545/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {11% of the time, Black replies with 1...d5, The Scandivanian Defense, and it was played in more than 400 million games. The main idea behind 1...d5 is to trade off the central pawns and get the queen out early so that Black can develop the queenside quickly, often choosing to castle long. We'll take the pawn.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen 72% of the time. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system, where we hope to develop with Be2, O-O, d4/c4 and so on. The Scandi has various setups for Black and general ideas to follow, but overall White's ideas are the same.} 3.Nf3 3...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight 22% of the time. We'll support and unpin the knight by developing our bishop to e2.} 4.Be2 4...Nc6 {In this setup, it's clear that Black is about to castle queenside, so there is no point in us going d4 to give them a target on the d-file. We'll just castle our king and go from there.} 5.O-O 5...O-O-O {Black castles their king. Let's ask the bishop a question now.} 6.h3 6...Bh5 {Black slides the bishop back to h5. We finally develop the queenside knight with tempo on Black's queen.} 7.Nc3 7...Qd7 {Black retreats the queen back to d7. We'll play the stunning b4! Let's see what Black wants to do.} 8.b4 8...Nxb4 {Black captures the pawn, but this is simply too dangerous! We jump our knight into e5, attacking Black's queen and opening up a discovered attack on Black's bishop!} 9.Ne5 9...Bxe2 {Black captures the bishop on e2. We recapture with the queen.} 10.Qxe2 10...Qe6 {Black moves the queen to e6. We continue with Rb1, attacking Black's knight and placing the rook on the semi-open b-file. White now has a very comfortable advantage and great attacking prospects on the queenside.} 11.Rb1 11...Nc6 {Another example that we can see is if the knight comes back to c6. We can give it up entirely for the attack on the b-file! That's how potent our b4 and b5 idea is.} 12.Qb5 12...Nxe5 {The knight takes on e5. You'd think Black is safe because b7 is only one pawn. The problem is the king isn't safe and the rook cannot defend by itself. Black's kingside is also out of the game.} 13.Qxb7+ 13...Kd7 {The king goes to d7. Without more material joining the attack, White wouldn't be doing well. But we've got the pawn!} 14.d4 14...Ng6 {The knight goes to g6. And we attack the queen, pushing with tempo.} 15.d5 15...Qd6 {The queen is the only defender, but it's easy to attack.} 16.Ne4 {We attack it, and force it away. This allows us to continue attacking the king and White is winning. All of this from our b4-idea!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Qxd5 and 3...Bg4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/39858176-3d3c-4c79-a05a-c58f68671545/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {11% of the time, Black replies with 1...d5, The Scandivanian Defense, and it was played in more than 400 million games. The main idea behind 1...d5 is to trade off the central pawns and get the queen out early so that Black can develop the queenside quickly, often choosing to castle long. We'll take the pawn.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen 72% of the time. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system, where we hope to develop with Be2, O-O, d4/c4 and so on. The Scandi has various setups for Black and general ideas to follow, but overall White's ideas are the same.} 3.Nf3 3...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight 22% of the time. We'll support and unpin the knight by developing our bishop to e2.} 4.Be2 4...Nc6 {In this setup, it's clear that Black is about to castle queenside, so there is no point in us going d4 to give them a target on the d-file. We'll just castle our king and go from there.} 5.O-O 5...O-O-O {Black castles their king. Let's ask the bishop a question now.} 6.h3 6...Bh5 {Black slides the bishop back to h5. We finally develop the queenside knight with tempo on Black's queen.} 7.Nc3 7...Qd7 {Black retreats the queen back to d7. We'll play the stunning b4! Let's see what Black wants to do.} 8.b4 8...Nxb4 {Black captures the pawn, but this is simply too dangerous! We jump our knight into e5, attacking Black's queen and opening up a discovered attack on Black's bishop!} 9.Ne5 9...Bxe2 {Black captures the bishop on e2. We recapture with the queen.} 10.Qxe2 10...Qe6 {Black moves the queen to e6. We continue with Rb1, attacking Black's knight and placing the rook on the semi-open b-file. White now has a very comfortable advantage and great attacking prospects on the queenside.} 11.Rb1 11...Nxc2 {The ambitious ...Nxc2 wins a pawn but doesn't work. We're winning.} 12.Qe4 {We have a double attack. After something like ...c6 there's the crazy Nb5. Black's king isn't safe and they're losing because of it.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Qxd5 and 3...Bg4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/39858176-3d3c-4c79-a05a-c58f68671545/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {11% of the time, Black replies with 1...d5, The Scandivanian Defense, and it was played in more than 400 million games. The main idea behind 1...d5 is to trade off the central pawns and get the queen out early so that Black can develop the queenside quickly, often choosing to castle long. We'll take the pawn.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen 72% of the time. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system, where we hope to develop with Be2, O-O, d4/c4 and so on. The Scandi has various setups for Black and general ideas to follow, but overall White's ideas are the same.} 3.Nf3 3...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight 22% of the time. We'll support and unpin the knight by developing our bishop to e2.} 4.Be2 4...Nc6 {In this setup, it's clear that Black is about to castle queenside, so there is no point in us going d4 to give them a target on the d-file. We'll just castle our king and go from there.} 5.O-O 5...O-O-O {Black castles their king. Let's ask the bishop a question now.} 6.h3 6...Bh5 {Black slides the bishop back to h5. We finally develop the queenside knight with tempo on Black's queen.} 7.Nc3 7...Qd7 {Black retreats the queen back to d7. We'll play the stunning b4! Let's see what Black wants to do.} 8.b4 8...e6 {Black doesn't capture the pawn, and instead continues with ...e6. We push our b-pawn up to b5 and attack Black's knight! That's our main idea if Black doesn't accept the b4-pawn.} 9.b5 9...Bxf3 {Black captures on f3. This move gives up the bishop pair but frees up the d4-square for the knight. We recapture on f3 with the bishop.} 10.Bxf3 10...Nd4 {Black jumps the knight into d4.} 11.Rb1 {And we support the b5-pawn with Rb1. White now has an objective edge and great attacking prospects on the queenside, and we'll look to continue with d3, a4 and Be3. A model game from this position is Ponomariov - Fressinet, 2016. Here's the link to that game: https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1816890} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Qxd5 and 3...Bg4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/39858176-3d3c-4c79-a05a-c58f68671545/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {11% of the time, Black replies with 1...d5, The Scandivanian Defense, and it was played in more than 400 million games. The main idea behind 1...d5 is to trade off the central pawns and get the queen out early so that Black can develop the queenside quickly, often choosing to castle long. We'll take the pawn.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen 72% of the time. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system, where we hope to develop with Be2, O-O, d4/c4 and so on. The Scandi has various setups for Black and general ideas to follow, but overall White's ideas are the same.} 3.Nf3 3...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight 22% of the time. We'll support and unpin the knight by developing our bishop to e2.} 4.Be2 4...Nc6 {In this setup, it's clear that Black is about to castle queenside, so there is no point in us going d4 to give them a target on the d-file. We'll just castle our king and go from there.} 5.O-O 5...O-O-O {Black castles their king. Let's ask the bishop a question now.} 6.h3 6...Bh5 {Black slides the bishop back to h5. We finally develop the queenside knight with tempo on Black's queen.} 7.Nc3 7...Qa5 {Black moves the queen over to a5. I had once recommended 8.a3 here, but 8.b4 is even stronger. The idea is to sacrifice the b-pawn and open up the b-file since Black has committed to the queenside.} 8.b4 8...Nxb4 {Having the knight capture on b4 is the most natural. That way, Rb1 doesn't come with a tempo. We get our rook on the b-file anyway.} 9.Rb1 9...Nxa2 {This continuation is very greedy. Our double attack works.} 10.Rb5 10...Nxc3 {This capture comes with an attack on the queen. But we're attacking the bishop too!} 11.Rxa5 11...Nxd1 {Black takes our queen.} 12.Rxh5 {And the knight is trapped on d1. We will go up material.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Qxd5 and 3...Bg4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/39858176-3d3c-4c79-a05a-c58f68671545/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {11% of the time, Black replies with 1...d5, The Scandivanian Defense, and it was played in more than 400 million games. The main idea behind 1...d5 is to trade off the central pawns and get the queen out early so that Black can develop the queenside quickly, often choosing to castle long. We'll take the pawn.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen 72% of the time. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system, where we hope to develop with Be2, O-O, d4/c4 and so on. The Scandi has various setups for Black and general ideas to follow, but overall White's ideas are the same.} 3.Nf3 3...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight 22% of the time. We'll support and unpin the knight by developing our bishop to e2.} 4.Be2 4...Nc6 {In this setup, it's clear that Black is about to castle queenside, so there is no point in us going d4 to give them a target on the d-file. We'll just castle our king and go from there.} 5.O-O 5...O-O-O {Black castles their king. Let's ask the bishop a question now.} 6.h3 6...Bh5 {Black slides the bishop back to h5. We finally develop the queenside knight with tempo on Black's queen.} 7.Nc3 7...Qa5 {Black moves the queen over to a5. I had once recommended 8.a3 here, but 8.b4 is even stronger. The idea is to sacrifice the b-pawn and open up the b-file since Black has committed to the queenside.} 8.b4 8...Nxb4 {Having the knight capture on b4 is the most natural. That way, Rb1 doesn't come with a tempo. We get our rook on the b-file anyway.} 9.Rb1 9...e6 {The move ...e6 lends some support to the knight. It's time for us to make use of our b-file pressure. So, we'll attack the knight.} 10.a3 10...Nd5 {The knight goes to d5 where it hopes to obstruct the efficacy of our Rb5 move. We'll take.} 11.Nxd5 11...Qxd5 {The queen recaptures. But all our moves come with tempi. The c-pawn advances first to grab more central space.} 12.c4 12...Qc6 {The queen goes to the c6-square.} 13.d4 {But our attack plays itself. Black won't have the answer to the upcoming aggression. We are looking to play d5 and place our queen and the bishop on b3 and e3. We will attack on the b-file and down the diagonal.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 2...Qxd5 and 3...Bg4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/39858176-3d3c-4c79-a05a-c58f68671545/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {11% of the time, Black replies with 1...d5, The Scandivanian Defense, and it was played in more than 400 million games. The main idea behind 1...d5 is to trade off the central pawns and get the queen out early so that Black can develop the queenside quickly, often choosing to castle long. We'll take the pawn.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen 72% of the time. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system, where we hope to develop with Be2, O-O, d4/c4 and so on. The Scandi has various setups for Black and general ideas to follow, but overall White's ideas are the same.} 3.Nf3 3...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight 22% of the time. We'll support and unpin the knight by developing our bishop to e2.} 4.Be2 4...Nc6 {In this setup, it's clear that Black is about to castle queenside, so there is no point in us going d4 to give them a target on the d-file. We'll just castle our king and go from there.} 5.O-O 5...O-O-O {Black castles their king. Let's ask the bishop a question now.} 6.h3 6...Bh5 {Black slides the bishop back to h5. We finally develop the queenside knight with tempo on Black's queen.} 7.Nc3 7...Qa5 {Black moves the queen over to a5. I had once recommended 8.a3 here, but 8.b4 is even stronger. The idea is to sacrifice the b-pawn and open up the b-file since Black has committed to the queenside.} 8.b4 8...Qxb4 {This move willingly puts the queen on the open file where it will be attacked. We are now enjoying b-file compensation.} 9.Rb1 9...Qa5 {If the queen goes back to a5, we can make use of the b-file.} 10.Rb5 {We're cashing in with this double attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nf6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/c5ef3174-141c-4ed6-9d59-03387fff1e29/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system.} 3.Nf3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight just over 10% of the time, in almost 2.5 million games. The best answer to this is the central expansion with d4. Black isn't as quick to develop and castle queenside, so we can afford to play this move.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight. Black often goes back to this idea. We will unpin, naturally.} 5.Be2 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight and prepares to castle queenside. We are okay with this and have time to castle.} 6.O-O 6...O-O-O {Black castles their king and threatens to capture the d4-pawn. That's why we will support the d-pawn by developing our dark-squared bishop to e3. Keep in mind that once Black lines up on the d-file and targets our d4-pawn, we have to consider ...e5 as an option at all times.} 7.Be3 7...e5 {Black does strike in the center with ...e5. We'll grab a tempo on the queen with c4.} 8.c4 8...Qd7 {Black can choose to go back to d7. In that case, d5 is a logical move to avoid confrontation in the center and attack the knight.} 9.d5 9...Bxf3 {Black can throw in this capture on f3. We'll simply recapture, keeping the bishop pair. Black still has to move away.} 10.Bxf3 10...Ne7 {Once they do, the a7-pawn is undefended. We'll take it.} 11.Bxa7 11...b6 {Black tries to trap our bishop, but we were ready for this. We'll develop the knight.} 12.Nc3 12...Kb7 {Black goes after the bishop. We can't save it, but we can use this time to try and open up the a-file, which is why we play a4.} 13.a4 13...Kxa7 {Black takes.} 14.a5 {We're coming to b5 no matter what, with a winning advantage. Our attack is strong and we have enough compensation for the bishop. The Black king is weak and the a-file will be opened soon.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nf6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/c5ef3174-141c-4ed6-9d59-03387fff1e29/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system.} 3.Nf3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight just over 10% of the time, in almost 2.5 million games. The best answer to this is the central expansion with d4. Black isn't as quick to develop and castle queenside, so we can afford to play this move.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight. Black often goes back to this idea. We will unpin, naturally.} 5.Be2 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight and prepares to castle queenside. We are okay with this and have time to castle.} 6.O-O 6...O-O-O {Black castles their king and threatens to capture the d4-pawn. That's why we will support the d-pawn by developing our dark-squared bishop to e3. Keep in mind that once Black lines up on the d-file and targets our d4-pawn, we have to consider ...e5 as an option at all times.} 7.Be3 7...e5 {Black does strike in the center with ...e5. We'll grab a tempo on the queen with c4.} 8.c4 8...Qa5 {Black moves the queen over to a5. This signals that they don't want to commit to attacking the kingside.} 9.d5 {We'll push the d-pawn up to d5. This shuts down the d-file pressure and keeps our position safer.} 9...e4 {Black might try to push ...e4 and attack our knight, since our d-pawn is pinned. But we can simply jump to g5, attack the f7-pawn and offer a trade of bishops.} 10.Ng5 10...Bxe2 {Black takes, trying to win time. We recapture. Now our d-pawn isn't pinned anymore and we are attacking both the knight and the f7-pawn.} 11.Qxe2 11...Ne5 {Black centralizes, defending f7. But we are persistent in attacking the defender of the f7-pawn. Also, now both our knight and the queen attack the e4-pawn.} 12.Bd4 12...h6 {Black counterattacks our knight. But we can win the pawn on e4.} 13.Nxe4 13...Nxe4 {Black trades.} 14.Bxe5 {Black is in a lot of trouble. They are down material and we control the center. This should be easy to convert.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nf6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/c5ef3174-141c-4ed6-9d59-03387fff1e29/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system.} 3.Nf3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight just over 10% of the time, in almost 2.5 million games. The best answer to this is the central expansion with d4. Black isn't as quick to develop and castle queenside, so we can afford to play this move.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight. Black often goes back to this idea. We will unpin, naturally.} 5.Be2 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight and prepares to castle queenside. We are okay with this and have time to castle.} 6.O-O 6...O-O-O {Black castles their king and threatens to capture the d4-pawn. That's why we will support the d-pawn by developing our dark-squared bishop to e3. Keep in mind that once Black lines up on the d-file and targets our d4-pawn, we have to consider ...e5 as an option at all times.} 7.Be3 7...e5 {Black does strike in the center with ...e5. We'll grab a tempo on the queen with c4.} 8.c4 8...Qe4 {If the queen moves up, it still exerts pressure on d4. The rook is also on the d-file, x-raying our queen. That's why we should develop our knight, attacking the queen.} 9.Nbd2 9...Qg6 {The queen goes to g6. Black has pressure on d4, so we have to deal with it. The best way to do this is by pushing the pawn and attacking the knight.} 10.d5 10...Bxf3 {The bishop takes on f3. We'll recapture with the knight.} 11.Nxf3 11...e4 {You may actually see this a lot in the Scandinavian: since the queen wanders off, it often runs out of squares.} 12.Nh4 {I wouldn't be surprised if you find yourself winning a queen by simply being solid and developing your pieces, as it's trapped here. Queens wandering off in the opening are a bad idea for beginners and intermediates.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nf6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/c5ef3174-141c-4ed6-9d59-03387fff1e29/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system.} 3.Nf3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight just over 10% of the time, in almost 2.5 million games. The best answer to this is the central expansion with d4. Black isn't as quick to develop and castle queenside, so we can afford to play this move.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight. Black often goes back to this idea. We will unpin, naturally.} 5.Be2 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight and prepares to castle queenside. We are okay with this and have time to castle.} 6.O-O 6...O-O-O {Black castles their king and threatens to capture the d4-pawn. That's why we will support the d-pawn by developing our dark-squared bishop to e3. Keep in mind that once Black lines up on the d-file and targets our d4-pawn, we have to consider ...e5 as an option at all times.} 7.Be3 7...e5 {Black does strike in the center with ...e5. We'll grab a tempo on the queen with c4.} 8.c4 8...Qe4 {If the queen moves up, it still exerts pressure on d4. The rook is also on the d-file, x-raying our queen. That's why we should develop our knight, attacking the queen.} 9.Nbd2 9...Qf5 {This is the queen's other option, but it doesn't change much. First, we make sure the d-file remains closed a bit longer by pushing the pawn.} 10.d5 10...Nb4 {If Black wants to continue in aggressive fashion, they will jump to b4, looking at the c2-square.} 11.h3 {And now we can attack the bishop on g4. ...Bxh3 doesn't work because we control over the center and we have enough pieces to defend the kingside. Black's attempts have failed and we are much more active and developed with a borderline winning advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nf6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/c5ef3174-141c-4ed6-9d59-03387fff1e29/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system.} 3.Nf3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight just over 10% of the time, in almost 2.5 million games. The best answer to this is the central expansion with d4. Black isn't as quick to develop and castle queenside, so we can afford to play this move.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight. Black often goes back to this idea. We will unpin, naturally.} 5.Be2 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight and prepares to castle queenside. We are okay with this and have time to castle.} 6.O-O 6...O-O-O {Black castles their king and threatens to capture the d4-pawn. That's why we will support the d-pawn by developing our dark-squared bishop to e3. Keep in mind that once Black lines up on the d-file and targets our d4-pawn, we have to consider ...e5 as an option at all times.} 7.Be3 7...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop. We should play h3 to attack the bishop and entice Black to move to the h-file. It's very important that if 8...h5 you remember the simple 9.Nbd2 and simply develop.} 8.h3 8...Bh5 {There's a chance Black won't play ...h5 but rather move the bishop to the h5-square. After the bishop goes to h5, I like attacking the queen because it has less options on the 5th rank.} 9.c4 9...Qd7 {If ...Qd7, the queen lacks much activity. It looks like the safest square, so that's why people may play it. We can simply continue development.} 10.Nbd2 10...h6 {Black covers their g5-square, but also creates a square for the bishop. We'll start focusing on the queenside, which is where our attacking prospects lie. The move a3 prepares b4.} 11.a3 11...g5 {Black may try to attack your kingside here. But without fear, we continue on the queenside! This expansion with b4 is crucial.} 12.b4 12...g4 {Black attacks the knight and the h3-hook. Jumping to e5 is fine here and allowing Black to take on h3 if they want to.} 13.Ne5 13...Nxe5 {Black trades the knights first. We'll recapture, without being afraid of any d-file pressure.} 14.dxe5 14...gxh3 {This capture won't do Black any good, as they are sacrificing the bishop. They'll lose material without a real attack. We can take the bishop.} 15.Bxh5 15...hxg2 {This desperado won't amount to anything.} 16.Re1 {Obviously we cannot take the pawn because we'd be exposed on the g-file. We're up material and should win easily.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nf6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/c5ef3174-141c-4ed6-9d59-03387fff1e29/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system.} 3.Nf3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight just over 10% of the time, in almost 2.5 million games. The best answer to this is the central expansion with d4. Black isn't as quick to develop and castle queenside, so we can afford to play this move.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight. Black often goes back to this idea. We will unpin, naturally.} 5.Be2 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight and prepares to castle queenside. We are okay with this and have time to castle.} 6.O-O 6...O-O-O {Black castles their king and threatens to capture the d4-pawn. That's why we will support the d-pawn by developing our dark-squared bishop to e3. Keep in mind that once Black lines up on the d-file and targets our d4-pawn, we have to consider ...e5 as an option at all times.} 7.Be3 7...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop. We should play h3 to attack the bishop and entice Black to move to the h-file. It's very important that if 8...h5 you remember the simple 9.Nbd2 and simply develop.} 8.h3 8...Bh5 {There's a chance Black won't play ...h5 but rather move the bishop to the h5-square. After the bishop goes to h5, I like attacking the queen because it has less options on the 5th rank.} 9.c4 9...Qf5 {If the queen goes to f5, this is a much more aggressive position for the queen. We'll develop our knight to c3. All normal thus far.} 10.Nc3 10...Bd6 {Black adds another piece eyeing the kingside, but we are well protected. We'll place our queen on a4, playing on the queenside. Our queen puts pressure on some weaker points and is ready to slide to the fifth rank if needed.} 11.Qa4 11...g5 {Black wants to go all in on the kingside. But we can even let them push ...g4! We'll start our own pressure on the queenside.} 12.b4 12...g4 {Continuing to push isn't good.} 13.Nh4 {The queen is trapped.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nf6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/c5ef3174-141c-4ed6-9d59-03387fff1e29/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system.} 3.Nf3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight just over 10% of the time, in almost 2.5 million games. The best answer to this is the central expansion with d4. Black isn't as quick to develop and castle queenside, so we can afford to play this move.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight. Black often goes back to this idea. We will unpin, naturally.} 5.Be2 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight and prepares to castle queenside. We are okay with this and have time to castle.} 6.O-O 6...O-O-O {Black castles their king and threatens to capture the d4-pawn. That's why we will support the d-pawn by developing our dark-squared bishop to e3. Keep in mind that once Black lines up on the d-file and targets our d4-pawn, we have to consider ...e5 as an option at all times.} 7.Be3 7...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop. We should play h3 to attack the bishop and entice Black to move to the h-file. It's very important that if 8...h5 you remember the simple 9.Nbd2 and simply develop.} 8.h3 8...Bh5 {There's a chance Black won't play ...h5 but rather move the bishop to the h5-square. After the bishop goes to h5, I like attacking the queen because it has less options on the 5th rank.} 9.c4 9...Qf5 {If the queen goes to f5, this is a much more aggressive position for the queen. We'll develop our knight to c3. All normal thus far.} 10.Nc3 10...Bd6 {Black adds another piece eyeing the kingside, but we are well protected. We'll place our queen on a4, playing on the queenside. Our queen puts pressure on some weaker points and is ready to slide to the fifth rank if needed.} 11.Qa4 11...g5 {Black wants to go all in on the kingside. But we can even let them push ...g4! We'll start our own pressure on the queenside.} 12.b4 12...Rhg8 {If Black wants to avoid trapping their own queen, they can play a move like ...Rhg8. But the attack feels slower now and we can continue on the queenside with c5! Counterplay on the queenside not only helps our attacking chances, but also tries to push Black's pieces away from attacking us.} 13.c5 13...Bxf3 {Supposing that Black takes on f3 in an attempt to speed up their attack, this won't work. We'll recapture, and since we've invested enough time on the queenside, we're quick enough.} 14.Bxf3 14...g4 {Black goes all in with ...g4 now, but we've made sufficient preparations against this attack. We'll take on c6 so that Black doesn't have a chance to capture our bishop.} 15.Bxc6 15...gxh3 {Black opens up the g-file. However, our bishop is still defending the g2-square. We'll push g3 though, to keep our position safer.} 16.g3 16...Bxg3 {Sure, Black can sacrifice. We'll accept.} 17.fxg3 17...Rxg3+ {But the trick in this position is that our light squares are safe.} 18.Kh1 {We will defend our light squares with the queen or bishop on c6. If Black goes all in and you've made enough progress on the queenside, you should be able to hold and even win if they attack unwisely. This position is winning for White.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nf6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/c5ef3174-141c-4ed6-9d59-03387fff1e29/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system.} 3.Nf3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight just over 10% of the time, in almost 2.5 million games. The best answer to this is the central expansion with d4. Black isn't as quick to develop and castle queenside, so we can afford to play this move.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight. Black often goes back to this idea. We will unpin, naturally.} 5.Be2 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight and prepares to castle queenside. We are okay with this and have time to castle.} 6.O-O 6...O-O-O {Black castles their king and threatens to capture the d4-pawn. That's why we will support the d-pawn by developing our dark-squared bishop to e3. Keep in mind that once Black lines up on the d-file and targets our d4-pawn, we have to consider ...e5 as an option at all times.} 7.Be3 7...Qf5 {This move applies more pressure on the knight, but it also makes the rook more effective on the d-file. Bringing out our other knight to d2 helps us block the d-file and overprotect our knight.} 8.Nbd2 8...e5 {The ...e5 break is always critical for Black. But as things stand, we're equipped to deal with it safely. We'll take on e5.} 9.Nxe5 9...Bxe2 {This initiates some trades. We recapture. Our queen is on the e-file now, so Black's ...e5 push lacks utility.} 10.Qxe2 10...Nxe5 {Black trades on e5. But this shouldn't be a problem at all.} 11.dxe5 11...Qxe5 {The queen takes.} 12.Nf3 {And we improve our knight. ...Qxb2 is simply bad for Black as Rab1 activates a rook on the b-file. We want to improve our position with moves like Re1 and Bd4 when ready.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nf6 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/c5ef3174-141c-4ed6-9d59-03387fff1e29/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system.} 3.Nf3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight just over 10% of the time, in almost 2.5 million games. The best answer to this is the central expansion with d4. Black isn't as quick to develop and castle queenside, so we can afford to play this move.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight. Black often goes back to this idea. We will unpin, naturally.} 5.Be2 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight and prepares to castle queenside. We are okay with this and have time to castle.} 6.O-O 6...e6 {Black could opt to play passively and just aim to develop the bishop. This allows us to continue development, too. We support the center.} 7.Be3 7...Bd6 {Black's bishop comes out. This was the point of ...e6. We can't say there's significant pressure on the kingside yet, but it could be on the cards. We should attack the bishop on g4.} 8.h3 8...h5 {Black may sacrifice. This is a common idea. Black wants to open up the h-file for a checkmating attack if we take. That's why we won't take and we'll wait actively by developing a piece.} 9.Nbd2 9...O-O-O {Black castles. Two rooks could potentially attack down the h-file now. We're still slowly improving our position, this time with Re1. This is also important because we open up the f1-square for our king if needed.} 10.Re1 10...g5 {Black may try pushing more pawns on the kingside. But now it's rather safe to take. We've cleared the f1-square, so our king isn't mated.} 11.hxg4 11...hxg4 {The h-file is now open but the queen cannot get to it because of the g-pawn. Meanwhile, we can win a tempo on the queen. We could have utilized this move earlier but we chose the right timing.} 12.Bc4 12...Qa5 {Since it cannot go to the kingside, it must go to a5! The knight is protected by the bishop and we can win a pawn.} 13.Nxg5 13...Bh2+ {Check.} 14.Kf1 {But we have f1. We're attacking f7 and we're much better here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nf6 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/c5ef3174-141c-4ed6-9d59-03387fff1e29/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. Against the Scandinavian, I'm going to recommend the flexible Nf3 system.} 3.Nf3 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight just over 10% of the time, in almost 2.5 million games. The best answer to this is the central expansion with d4. Black isn't as quick to develop and castle queenside, so we can afford to play this move.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight. Black often goes back to this idea. We will unpin, naturally.} 5.Be2 5...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop. We'll simply castle.} 6.O-O 6...Be7 {Black develops the bishop to e7 now. It's time for us to attack the bishop.} 7.h3 7...Bh5 {Black slides the bishop back to h5. There are no ...h5 ideas in this lime since Black might want to castle kingside. A healthy idea to attack the queen before we develop the knight to c3 is the move c4. We push the queen away and grab some central space in one move.} 8.c4 8...Qd8 {Black retreats the queen back to d8.} 9.Qb3 {And we'll move our queen out to b3 and attack Black's b7-pawn. White now has a very comfortable position with easy development, such as Nc3 with the bishop on e3. The rook can slide to d1.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6 and 2...c6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/cd2aaffe-5019-4d7f-9ac2-c8e6a9f20604/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Nf6 {Black doesn't recapture with the queen, and instead tries to avoid losing the lead in development by attacking the d5-pawn with the knight. This happens almost 20% of the time and in more than 50 million games. We'll pretty much ignore Black's move and continue with our standard plan involving Be2, O-O, and d4/c4.} 3.Nf3 3...Nxd5 {Black captures the pawn on d5. We should play d4 quickly if allowed.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight. We support and unpin the knight by developing our bishop to e2. White should look to continue with O-O, c4 and Nc3.} 5.Be2 5...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. We'll castle. This is one of the benefits of our Nf3 variation: we get to castle quickly.} 6.O-O 6...Be7 {Black develops their dark-squared bishop. And we attack the g4-bishop, trying to get the bishop pair.} 7.h3 7...Bh5 {Black retreats. 8.c4 is a good move because we force the knight away from its active d5-home. Once we've completed kingside development and castled, we start to push Black's pieces away. Now all that's left is improvement.} 8.c4 8...Nb6 {Black can also go to b6. This is a passive square for the knight, but there isn't an immediate way to punish it. The best we can do is solidify our center and develop our pieces behind the pawns.} 9.b3 9...O-O {Black castles. They lack a lot of space and the bishops aren't on good squares. We'll develop our other knight.} 10.Nc3 10...Nc6 {Black brings their knight out as well. When we have a strong center, we don't always want to push our pawns forward. It's sometimes better to defend them to keep control of many squares, like we do here with Be3.} 11.Be3 11...Bf6 {Black applies pressure on d4. Which is why we have to look to get rid of an attacker.} 12.Ne4 12...Bxf3 {Black trades. We cannot recapture the bishop right away because of the d4-pawn. So we first remove the defender with check.} 13.Nxf6+ 13...Qxf6 {The queen recaptures. And now we take back on f3.} 14.Bxf3 14...Rad8 {Black gets some pressure on d4. This forces us to part ways with our bishop pair. We simply have to remove some attackers in order to keep our center intact.} 15.Bxc6 15...bxc6 {After the recapture, we get some valuable time to get out of the d-file.} 16.Qc2 {We're going to centralize both rooks soon. We have a positional edge because of our better pawn structure and the bishop which is better than Black's knight. Our central pawn duo controls critical squares in the position and Black lacks squares for their pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6 and 2...c6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/cd2aaffe-5019-4d7f-9ac2-c8e6a9f20604/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...c6 {This is called the Blackburne-Kloosterboer Gambit and it's played 4% of the time. Black wants to gambit a pawn in order to accelerate their development. This isn't the best choice if we know how to fight it. We'll accept.} 3.dxc6 3...Nxc6 {Black gets to develop their knight quickly. Let's get our knight out, too.} 4.Nf3 4...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn. An idea could be ...e4. That's why we'll develop the queenside knight to c3.} 5.Nc3 5...Nf6 {Black might go for the four knights formation. We'll stick to our principles and pin the knight. We attack e5 and get ready to castle.} 6.Bb5 6...Bd6 {After the bishop comes to d6, we can think of pushing our d-pawn. With the bishop on d6, there wouldn't be a queen trade after the trades on d4. Furthermore, we open up the e-file for our rook.} 7.d4 7...e4 {The most natural reaction involves pushing the e-pawn. We can jump to e5 and attack the pinned knight.} 8.Ne5 8...Bxe5 {One of the solutions to the problem posed by Ne5 is to take the knight. However, the solution makes the problem worse: we get a pawn on e5 attacking Black's knight.} 9.dxe5 9...Qxd1+ {Even though Black can trade, the position is better for us. We recapture with our knight. We have an extra pawn and a better position. When someone plays a gambit, their aim is to get an initiative. After the queens are traded, the gambit was all for nothing.} 10.Nxd1 10...Ng4 {Black tries to move away and attack the e5-pawn. But we're right there to defend.} 11.Bf4 11...Bd7 {The bishop goes to d7. We can even afford to take on c6. The bishop won't be active on c6, as it's staring at e4.} 12.Bxc6 12...Bxc6 {White recaptures. We have to blockade the pawn, because Black gets to activate otherwise. But first we'll attack the knight.} 13.h3 13...Nh6 {Black escapes to h6.} 14.Ne3 {And on e3 we make sure that the e-pawn isn't pushed and that the c6-bishop remains useless. We can choose either side of the board to castle on the next move. We've kept our extra pawn and Black has no compensation for it. Their pieces aren't active at all, and we have more space with the pawn on e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6 and 2...c6 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/cd2aaffe-5019-4d7f-9ac2-c8e6a9f20604/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...c6 {This is called the Blackburne-Kloosterboer Gambit and it's played 4% of the time. Black wants to gambit a pawn in order to accelerate their development. This isn't the best choice if we know how to fight it. We'll accept.} 3.dxc6 3...Nxc6 {Black gets to develop their knight quickly. Let's get our knight out, too.} 4.Nf3 4...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn. An idea could be ...e4. That's why we'll develop the queenside knight to c3.} 5.Nc3 5...Bc5 {Black can develop their bishop to c5. An aggressive move because they aim at f2. However, we have this strong bishop pin which also allows us to castle next.} 6.Bb5 6...Ne7 {Black reinforces their knight, and it doesn't make much sense to take on c6 anymore. We also cannot really take on e5 because Black has ...Bxf2+ tactics. Hence we'll play 7.h3. The idea is to prevent ...Bg4, limiting the good squares the c8-bishop can go to.} 7.h3 7...O-O {Black castles. And so do we.} 8.O-O 8...a6 {Black tries to get us to take on c6. But since we don't have to take, we can simply go to a4.} 9.Ba4 9...b5 {Black pushes their b-pawn, and the idea is to fianchetto and win a tempo on the way. We go to b3 and our bishop sits on a long diagonal. This powerful bishop pins the f7-pawn to the king. If I were Black, I'd think about ...Na5 to remove that piece.} 10.Bb3 10...Bb7 {However, it's probable that Black will simply develop their bishop. This is where we can start to pose some serious problems. Since Black has focused on queenside development, we can utilize that and try to organize a quick attack on the kingside.} 11.Ng5 11...h6 {Things can go south for Black fairly quickly. All of a sudden, our queen is active on the h-file. It may seem like we lack an attacker here...} 12.Qh5 12...hxg5 {But once Black takes on g5... We can add the knight to the attack with tempo! The move ...g6 is bad because of Qh6 and Nf6# ideas, coupled with the weak pawn on g6.} 13.Ne4 13...Bb6 {If Black tries to preserve their material, our knight arrives.} 14.Nxg5 {There is forced checkmate in this position. Black cannot escape because our queen, knight and light-squared bishop converge on the key escape squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6 and 2...c6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/cd2aaffe-5019-4d7f-9ac2-c8e6a9f20604/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...c6 {This is called the Blackburne-Kloosterboer Gambit and it's played 4% of the time. Black wants to gambit a pawn in order to accelerate their development. This isn't the best choice if we know how to fight it. We'll accept.} 3.dxc6 3...Nxc6 {Black gets to develop their knight quickly. Let's get our knight out, too.} 4.Nf3 4...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn. An idea could be ...e4. That's why we'll develop the queenside knight to c3.} 5.Nc3 5...Nf6 {Black might go for the four knights formation. We'll stick to our principles and pin the knight. We attack e5 and get ready to castle.} 6.Bb5 6...Bd6 {After the bishop comes to d6, we can think of pushing our d-pawn. With the bishop on d6, there wouldn't be a queen trade after the trades on d4. Furthermore, we open up the e-file for our rook.} 7.d4 7...e4 {The most natural reaction involves pushing the e-pawn. We can jump to e5 and attack the pinned knight.} 8.Ne5 8...O-O {If Black castles, we can start trading some pieces and removing the sting from Black's gambit. An easy move: we get rid of our bad bishop and worsen Black's pawn structure.} 9.Bxc6 9...bxc6 {Black recaptures. They now have an isolated c-pawn which is a clear weakness.} 10.Bg5 {We don't have to take on c6 right away. We can pin the knight and increase our pressure within the position. Up a pawn with a better position, we have a slight edge. Black's loose pawns start to come to light here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6 and 2...c6 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/cd2aaffe-5019-4d7f-9ac2-c8e6a9f20604/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...c6 {This is called the Blackburne-Kloosterboer Gambit and it's played 4% of the time. Black wants to gambit a pawn in order to accelerate their development. This isn't the best choice if we know how to fight it. We'll accept.} 3.dxc6 3...Nxc6 {Black gets to develop their knight quickly. Let's get our knight out, too.} 4.Nf3 4...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn. An idea could be ...e4. That's why we'll develop the queenside knight to c3.} 5.Nc3 5...Nf6 {Black might go for the four knights formation. We'll stick to our principles and pin the knight. We attack e5 and get ready to castle.} 6.Bb5 6...Bd6 {After the bishop comes to d6, we can think of pushing our d-pawn. With the bishop on d6, there wouldn't be a queen trade after the trades on d4. Furthermore, we open up the e-file for our rook.} 7.d4 7...O-O {If you see that Black quickly castles, they are trying to do two things: 1) Unpin the knight on c6 to attack the pawn. 2) Bring the rook to the e-file to attack our king. Therefore we will take on c6 to get rid of these threats for one move and set up a little trap.} 8.Bxc6 8...bxc6 {A natural move like this is a huge blunder. We get a free fork. Black had to play 8...e4, but very few players find that solution. Capturing on d5 wins a piece.} 9.dxe5 9...Re8 {This is a temporary pin on the e-pawn.} 10.O-O {But we can castle and get out of the way. We will win a piece because after ...Bxe5 we can trade queens on d8 and then recapture on e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6 and 2...c6 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/cd2aaffe-5019-4d7f-9ac2-c8e6a9f20604/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...c6 {This is called the Blackburne-Kloosterboer Gambit and it's played 4% of the time. Black wants to gambit a pawn in order to accelerate their development. This isn't the best choice if we know how to fight it. We'll accept.} 3.dxc6 3...Nxc6 {Black gets to develop their knight quickly. Let's get our knight out, too.} 4.Nf3 4...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn. An idea could be ...e4. That's why we'll develop the queenside knight to c3.} 5.Nc3 5...Nf6 {Black might go for the four knights formation. We'll stick to our principles and pin the knight. We attack e5 and get ready to castle.} 6.Bb5 6...Bd6 {After the bishop comes to d6, we can think of pushing our d-pawn. With the bishop on d6, there wouldn't be a queen trade after the trades on d4. Furthermore, we open up the e-file for our rook.} 7.d4 7...exd4 {Black can also take on d4. We can take on c6 with check. We will take back on d4 next and have a piece on the strong d4-square.} 8.Bxc6+ 8...bxc6 {Black recaptures with a weak c6-pawn now. And our queen stands on d4, controlling many squares around the board.} 9.Qxd4 9...O-O {Black castles. And so do we.} 10.O-O 10...Bg4 {Black attacks our knight. But it really isn't a threat: we have a move to fight back.} 11.Bf4 {We attack the bishop on d6. If ...Bxf3, we win the bishop on d6. And if not, we will get the queen to f4 and defend our knight. Anyhow, we have more space and better squares for our pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6 and 2...c6 / Variation 13"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/cd2aaffe-5019-4d7f-9ac2-c8e6a9f20604/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...c6 {This is called the Blackburne-Kloosterboer Gambit and it's played 4% of the time. Black wants to gambit a pawn in order to accelerate their development. This isn't the best choice if we know how to fight it. We'll accept.} 3.dxc6 3...Nxc6 {Black gets to develop their knight quickly. Let's get our knight out, too.} 4.Nf3 4...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn. An idea could be ...e4. That's why we'll develop the queenside knight to c3.} 5.Nc3 5...f5 {An aggressive option is definitely ...f5, preparing ...e4. However, it's a bad move because it weakens Black's kingside and does nothing to develop more pieces. 6.Bb5 allows us to attack e5.} 6.Bb5 6...Bd6 {If ...Bd6 to defend, we've got a powerful move to break open the position. The d4-pawn break is designed to open up things for us because we are better developed.} 7.d4 7...e4 {Black has to keep things closed and they may push ...e4 to attack us. We can win a tempo on the queen first.} 8.Bg5 8...Nf6 {The knight blocks.} 9.Ne5 {And we centralize our knight while applying pressure on the pinned c6-knight. Black is much worse according to the engine. It's hard to glue their position together. No matter what, there will be big positional concessions.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6 and 2...c6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/cd2aaffe-5019-4d7f-9ac2-c8e6a9f20604/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Nf6 {Black doesn't recapture with the queen, and instead tries to avoid losing the lead in development by attacking the d5-pawn with the knight. This happens almost 20% of the time and in more than 50 million games. We'll pretty much ignore Black's move and continue with our standard plan involving Be2, O-O, and d4/c4.} 3.Nf3 3...Nxd5 {Black captures the pawn on d5. We should play d4 quickly if allowed.} 4.d4 4...Nc6 {The move 4...Nc6 is still very popular. Black wants to develop as much as they can before we push their pieces back. However, there are advantages to having a stronger center: we can bully Black's pieces away now.} 5.c4 5...Nf6 {The knight goes to f6. We're going to develop classically. Our idea is simple: d5.} 6.Nc3 6...Bg4 {Black goes for the pin, basically attacking the d4-pawn. But this plays into our hands as we push d5 an attack the knight! We're going to make use of our mobile pawn center and thus remove the attack on the d-pawn.} 7.d5 7...Ne5 {Black will most likely jump to e5. But we simply add a defender to f3, and we're already better in this position.} 8.Be2 8...Bxf3 {Black can trade on f3. The idea is that once we recapture with the bishop, they can take on c4.} 9.Bxf3 9...Nxc4 {If they do, make sure your tactics are sharp.} 10.Qa4+ {That's a free knight. Good game.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6 and 2...c6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/cd2aaffe-5019-4d7f-9ac2-c8e6a9f20604/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Nf6 {Black doesn't recapture with the queen, and instead tries to avoid losing the lead in development by attacking the d5-pawn with the knight. This happens almost 20% of the time and in more than 50 million games. We'll pretty much ignore Black's move and continue with our standard plan involving Be2, O-O, and d4/c4.} 3.Nf3 3...Nxd5 {Black captures the pawn on d5. We should play d4 quickly if allowed.} 4.d4 4...Nc6 {The move 4...Nc6 is still very popular. Black wants to develop as much as they can before we push their pieces back. However, there are advantages to having a stronger center: we can bully Black's pieces away now.} 5.c4 5...Nf6 {The knight goes to f6. We're going to develop classically. Our idea is simple: d5.} 6.Nc3 6...Bg4 {Black goes for the pin, basically attacking the d4-pawn. But this plays into our hands as we push d5 an attack the knight! We're going to make use of our mobile pawn center and thus remove the attack on the d-pawn.} 7.d5 7...Ne5 {Black will most likely jump to e5. But we simply add a defender to f3, and we're already better in this position.} 8.Be2 8...Nxf3+ {Black can trade on f3 with the knight. We'll recapture.} 9.Bxf3 9...Bxf3 {Black trades further. And we recapture.} 10.Qxf3 10...g6 {Because of our strong d5-pawn, Black has to develop through g7. We castle, with a nice edge in development.} 11.O-O 11...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes, ready to castle as well. And we'll place the rook on the open e-file. This will make the e7-pawn weaker, especially after Black castles.} 12.Re1 12...O-O {Black castles.} 13.Bf4 {Our rooks are connected and we have pressure both on c7 and e7. We're going to centralize our other rook and eventually organize our attack on one of the weak points of the position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6 and 2...c6 / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/cd2aaffe-5019-4d7f-9ac2-c8e6a9f20604/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...c6 {This is called the Blackburne-Kloosterboer Gambit and it's played 4% of the time. Black wants to gambit a pawn in order to accelerate their development. This isn't the best choice if we know how to fight it. We'll accept.} 3.dxc6 3...Nxc6 {Black gets to develop their knight quickly. Let's get our knight out, too.} 4.Nf3 4...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn. An idea could be ...e4. That's why we'll develop the queenside knight to c3.} 5.Nc3 5...f5 {An aggressive option is definitely ...f5, preparing ...e4. However, it's a bad move because it weakens Black's kingside and does nothing to develop more pieces. 6.Bb5 allows us to attack e5.} 6.Bb5 6...e4 {Black may push the e-pawn to attack the f3-knight. We can jump to e5 and put pressure on the pinned knight on c6. This is another reason behind Bb5.} 7.Ne5 7...Bd7 {Black brings their bishop out to support the c6-knight. We'll actually trade here, since our pieces are a bit loose.} 8.Bxc6 8...Bxc6 {Black recaptures. We'll take again.} 9.Nxc6 9...bxc6 {Black takes. We did this not only to remove the pieces off the board, but to leave Black with the weak and exposed pawn structure on the light squares.} 10.d3 {We can begin to chip away at the pawns. We are much better. Our idea is that if ...exd3, we will castle and play Re1+.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6 and 2...c6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/cd2aaffe-5019-4d7f-9ac2-c8e6a9f20604/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Nf6 {Black doesn't recapture with the queen, and instead tries to avoid losing the lead in development by attacking the d5-pawn with the knight. This happens almost 20% of the time and in more than 50 million games. We'll pretty much ignore Black's move and continue with our standard plan involving Be2, O-O, and d4/c4.} 3.Nf3 3...Nxd5 {Black captures the pawn on d5. We should play d4 quickly if allowed.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight. We support and unpin the knight by developing our bishop to e2. White should look to continue with O-O, c4 and Nc3.} 5.Be2 5...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. We'll castle. This is one of the benefits of our Nf3 variation: we get to castle quickly.} 6.O-O 6...Be7 {Black develops their dark-squared bishop. And we attack the g4-bishop, trying to get the bishop pair.} 7.h3 7...Bh5 {Black retreats. 8.c4 is a good move because we force the knight away from its active d5-home. Once we've completed kingside development and castled, we start to push Black's pieces away. Now all that's left is improvement.} 8.c4 8...Nf6 {Going back to f6 is the most natural. We'll develop our queenside knight and exert control over the central e4- and d5-squares.} 9.Nc3 9...O-O {Black castles. We'll centralize our rook. Improvement and development.} 10.Re1 10...c6 {With ...c6, Black achieves a standard Scandi configuration: the diamond pawn structure that controls d5.} 11.Bf4 {A solid way to handle it is to develop quickly and get the queen out of the d-file so that when Black eventually plays their rooks to d8 our d4-pawn isn't pinned. We have more space and a slightly better position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6 and 2...c6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/cd2aaffe-5019-4d7f-9ac2-c8e6a9f20604/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Nf6 {Black doesn't recapture with the queen, and instead tries to avoid losing the lead in development by attacking the d5-pawn with the knight. This happens almost 20% of the time and in more than 50 million games. We'll pretty much ignore Black's move and continue with our standard plan involving Be2, O-O, and d4/c4.} 3.Nf3 3...Nxd5 {Black captures the pawn on d5. We should play d4 quickly if allowed.} 4.d4 4...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5 instead of g4. In this case, we can offer a trade. We don't want Black's bishop to remain too active and this way we are ready to castle.} 5.Bd3 5...Bxd3 {The bishop takes on d3. We'll recapture with the queen. Our queen is active, and we've gotten rid of Black's developed bishop.} 6.Qxd3 6...e6 {This setup from Black isn't challenging at all. We'll even be able to kick the knight out of d5 with c4 at some point. But first, we'll get our king to safety, enjoying control of e5.} 7.O-O 7...Be7 {Many times, people in the Scandinavian like to have a solid position in which there's little danger and they can develop their pieces. We're okay with allowing them to have their solid position if it means we'll have more space and central control.} 8.c4 8...Nf6 {The knight goes back to f6. Our pieces are on great squares and controlling the center. Having more space also gives us room for our pieces.} 9.Nc3 9...O-O {Black castles.} 10.Bf4 {But if you observe the 4 squares in the center, it must be concluded that White is in control and that the game will be dictated by what we choose to do. We can centralize both rooks and enjoy almost perfect development.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Scandinavian Defense / Study 3: Black Plays 2...Nf6 and 2...c6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/836095d1-5997-46c5-b7ad-bc7733a64494/studies/cd2aaffe-5019-4d7f-9ac2-c8e6a9f20604/lines"]

1.e4 1...d5 {Black replies with 1...d5. We'll take.} 2.exd5 2...Nf6 {Black doesn't recapture with the queen, and instead tries to avoid losing the lead in development by attacking the d5-pawn with the knight. This happens almost 20% of the time and in more than 50 million games. We'll pretty much ignore Black's move and continue with our standard plan involving Be2, O-O, and d4/c4.} 3.Nf3 3...Bg4 {This pin transposes back to our 3...Nxd5 line. We'll unpin.} 4.Be2 4...Nxd5 {Black takes on d5.} 5.d4 {This is a transposition.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/80444ee0-58da-4520-9133-65cf4805bdc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black goes for the French 10% of the time, which is still almost 380 million games in the database! The pawn on e6 supports the d5-advancement, but blocks in the bishop on c8. We'll simply develop our knight for now.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And once Black goes for d5... We bring our other knight out. This is our starting position with the two knights on f3 and c3. In this study, we'll analyze the 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 responses.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the knight just over 20% of the time. In most positions like this, we will push the pawn to e5 to grab some more space and attack the knight. This makes our position easier to play and forces Black to rely on flank pawn breaks like ...c5.} 4.e5 4...Nfd7 {Black usually goes to d7. I now prefer d4, connecting the pawns.} 5.d4 5...c5 {Black plays ...c5, undermining our structure. This is the key move they have to know. I actually recommend capturing on c5 because we don't have time to defend the d4-pawn with another pawn.} 6.dxc5 6...Bxc5 {Black recaptures. Our bishop is well placed on the b1-h7 diagonal when the pawn is on e5.} 7.Bd3 7...Nc6 {Black develops, attacking e5. And we can develop, defending.} 8.Bf4 8...Qb6 {...Qb6 attacks both b2 and f2. This is the most popular move. We should defend the more important of the two pawns by castling and allow b2 to be taken! We have a better position and we'll prove it.} 9.O-O 9...Qxb2 {Black captures on b2 and attacks our knight. But we'll jump to b5 and threaten a fork on c7. It turns out that Black's queen has ventured too far from the rest of the Black pieces and can't defend.} 10.Nb5 10...O-O {If Black castles... There's a Greek Gift.} 11.Bxh7+ 11...Kxh7 {If the king takes, White is completely winning. Check!} 12.Ng5+ 12...Kg8 {The king goes back.} 13.Qd3 {And now, Qd3 so that Black doesn't play ...Qxc2. There's no way to prevent infiltration at this point. The evaluation is +4.2.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/80444ee0-58da-4520-9133-65cf4805bdc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black goes for the French 10% of the time, which is still almost 380 million games in the database! The pawn on e6 supports the d5-advancement, but blocks in the bishop on c8. We'll simply develop our knight for now.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And once Black goes for d5... We bring our other knight out. This is our starting position with the two knights on f3 and c3. In this study, we'll analyze the 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 responses.} 3.Nc3 3...d4 {Black pushes ...d4 just over a quarter of the time. This move, attacking the knight, is the second most popular move in the database. I suggest we bring our knight back, putting pressure on d4.} 4.Ne2 4...c5 {And by far the most popular continuation is ...c5, claiming that central space. It's best to immediately challenge the structure. The move ...d3 isn't possible because the pawn is too far advanced and it can't be defended after Nf4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {...Nc6 is the most popular move. Let's capture once.} 6.cxd4 6...cxd4 {Black recaptures. And now we can pin the knight, threatening to win the pawn.} 7.Qa4 7...Bc5 {...Bc5 is the most popular defense. Black simply adds a defender to the pawn. So how can we target that defender? With b4!} 8.b4 8...Bxb4 {The bishop takes. We've successfully distracted the bishop and now we can win the pawn on d4. This also pressures the knight on c6.} 9.Nexd4 9...Ne7 {Black will get the kingside knight to e7 at some point to help with the defense of the pinned knight on c6. In that case, it's best to develop with increasing pressure.} 10.Bb5 10...Qa5 {Black defends again and offers a trade of queens. I suggest we remain calm here and slide the rook to b1, attacking the bishop. This basically forces Black to trade.} 11.Rb1 11...Qxa4 {Black trades the queens.} 12.Bxa4 {And White has pressure and better pieces than Black. Just to illustrate the pressure, the bishop on b4 is under attack because its defender is pinned. If Black plays ...Bc5, Nxc6 is a tricky move to face. Because if Black recaptures with their knight, d4 for d5 is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/80444ee0-58da-4520-9133-65cf4805bdc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black goes for the French 10% of the time, which is still almost 380 million games in the database! The pawn on e6 supports the d5-advancement, but blocks in the bishop on c8. We'll simply develop our knight for now.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And once Black goes for d5... We bring our other knight out. This is our starting position with the two knights on f3 and c3. In this study, we'll analyze the 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 responses.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the knight just over 20% of the time. In most positions like this, we will push the pawn to e5 to grab some more space and attack the knight. This makes our position easier to play and forces Black to rely on flank pawn breaks like ...c5.} 4.e5 4...Nfd7 {Black usually goes to d7. I now prefer d4, connecting the pawns.} 5.d4 5...c5 {Black plays ...c5, undermining our structure. This is the key move they have to know. I actually recommend capturing on c5 because we don't have time to defend the d4-pawn with another pawn.} 6.dxc5 6...Bxc5 {Black recaptures. Our bishop is well placed on the b1-h7 diagonal when the pawn is on e5.} 7.Bd3 7...Nc6 {Black develops, attacking e5. And we can develop, defending.} 8.Bf4 8...O-O {Castling is also very popular, but never forget: The Greek Gift sacrifice is an idea whenever you've got a pawn on e5, a knight on f3 and a bishop on d3.} 9.Bxh7+ 9...Kxh7 {Just to demonstrate how a Greek Gift works, Black captures on h7. It involves the knight coming to g5 with check first.} 10.Ng5+ 10...Kg6 {And if the king refuses to go back... Check on d3!} 11.Qd3+ 11...f5 {Black has to play ...f5.} 12.Qg3 {And now we threaten all kinds of discovered attacks! White is winning here since Black's king is too exposed.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/80444ee0-58da-4520-9133-65cf4805bdc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black goes for the French 10% of the time, which is still almost 380 million games in the database! The pawn on e6 supports the d5-advancement, but blocks in the bishop on c8. We'll simply develop our knight for now.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And once Black goes for d5... We bring our other knight out. This is our starting position with the two knights on f3 and c3. In this study, we'll analyze the 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 responses.} 3.Nc3 3...d4 {Black pushes ...d4 just over a quarter of the time. This move, attacking the knight, is the second most popular move in the database. I suggest we bring our knight back, putting pressure on d4.} 4.Ne2 4...c5 {And by far the most popular continuation is ...c5, claiming that central space. It's best to immediately challenge the structure. The move ...d3 isn't possible because the pawn is too far advanced and it can't be defended after Nf4.} 5.c3 5...Nf6 {...Nf6 attacks the e4-pawn. Black is trying to complicate matters even more. Let's push and counterattack.} 6.e5 6...Nfd7 {Black keeps the pressure on e5. We'll take on d4 and it looks like we're winning a pawn.} 7.cxd4 7...cxd4 {Black recaptures. We'll take with the e-knight so that the f-knight still guards the e5-pawn.} 8.Nexd4 8...Nxe5 {But Black had this tactic in mind. The idea is that if Nxe5, Black has ...Qxd4. We'll give a check instead of falling for it. We are trying to prompt Black to place a piece on the d-file and block their own queen.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Nec6 {This knight must block instead. Now we can castle.} 10.O-O 10...Be7 {Black gets ready to castle too. We'll rotate the knight back to c2 in order to leave some space for the d4-thrust. That's how we will activate in the center of the board.} 11.Nc2 11...O-O {Black castles now. And we activate our isolated Queen's pawn.} 12.d4 12...a6 {If Black forces us away from the diagonal... There's no reason to trade on c6.} 13.Bd3 13...Nd7 {They develop their other knight. The best square for our queen is e2.} 14.Qe2 14...Nf6 {The knight comes to the kingside.} 15.Bf4 {And we develop our bishop, with an interesting game ahead that we'll play following the principles of IQP positions. We will try to protect our d4-pawn as much as we can and turn it into an asset down the line. On the other hand, Black will try to block it and leave it a liability for us.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/80444ee0-58da-4520-9133-65cf4805bdc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black goes for the French 10% of the time, which is still almost 380 million games in the database! The pawn on e6 supports the d5-advancement, but blocks in the bishop on c8. We'll simply develop our knight for now.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And once Black goes for d5... We bring our other knight out. This is our starting position with the two knights on f3 and c3. In this study, we'll analyze the 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 responses.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the knight just over 20% of the time. In most positions like this, we will push the pawn to e5 to grab some more space and attack the knight. This makes our position easier to play and forces Black to rely on flank pawn breaks like ...c5.} 4.e5 4...Nfd7 {Black usually goes to d7. I now prefer d4, connecting the pawns.} 5.d4 5...c5 {Black plays ...c5, undermining our structure. This is the key move they have to know. I actually recommend capturing on c5 because we don't have time to defend the d4-pawn with another pawn.} 6.dxc5 6...Nc6 {Black could delay capturing on c5, simply developing the knight. This is a sound approach, as it attacks the more important e5-pawn. We should defend this pawn, since in many positions it is a strong asset in our kingside attacks.} 7.Bf4 7...Nxc5 {And now instead of a transposition with ...Bxc5, Black could take on c5 with the knight, controlling some squares in our camp. But we should still put our bishop on d3, controlling the e4-square. We're not concerned about ...Nxd3.} 8.Bd3 8...Be7 {8...Be7 is the most natural continuation, clearing the way for castling. And our setup consists of Bd3, a3 and O-O. With a3, we're preventing ...Nb4, and now the way is clear for O-O.} 9.a3 9...O-O {Black usually castles here.} 10.O-O {And we'll castle as well. A common pawn break in these positions is ...f6 or ...f5. Against ...f6, we can play Re1 and recapture on e5 with the bishop. If Black plays ...f5, en passant and Bg3 are good ideas, with the Re1 idea always being a sound maneuver in these positions.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/80444ee0-58da-4520-9133-65cf4805bdc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black goes for the French 10% of the time, which is still almost 380 million games in the database! The pawn on e6 supports the d5-advancement, but blocks in the bishop on c8. We'll simply develop our knight for now.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And once Black goes for d5... We bring our other knight out. This is our starting position with the two knights on f3 and c3. In this study, we'll analyze the 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 responses.} 3.Nc3 3...d4 {Black pushes ...d4 just over a quarter of the time. This move, attacking the knight, is the second most popular move in the database. I suggest we bring our knight back, putting pressure on d4.} 4.Ne2 4...c5 {And by far the most popular continuation is ...c5, claiming that central space. It's best to immediately challenge the structure. The move ...d3 isn't possible because the pawn is too far advanced and it can't be defended after Nf4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {...Nc6 is the most popular move. Let's capture once.} 6.cxd4 6...cxd4 {Black recaptures. And now we can pin the knight, threatening to win the pawn.} 7.Qa4 7...Bc5 {...Bc5 is the most popular defense. Black simply adds a defender to the pawn. So how can we target that defender? With b4!} 8.b4 8...Bb6 {If the bishop goes back, we have a logical continuation. I really like b5, attacking the knight.} 9.b5 9...Nce7 {And once it retreats...} 10.Bb2 {Bb2 exerts great pressure on the pawns and the diagonal. Black will most likely continue with ...Nf6, attacking e4. But their coordination isn't the best, so their piece pressure isn't good either. If ...Nf6, Ng3 defends everything, and we're still prepared to play Nxd4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/80444ee0-58da-4520-9133-65cf4805bdc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black goes for the French 10% of the time, which is still almost 380 million games in the database! The pawn on e6 supports the d5-advancement, but blocks in the bishop on c8. We'll simply develop our knight for now.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And once Black goes for d5... We bring our other knight out. This is our starting position with the two knights on f3 and c3. In this study, we'll analyze the 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 responses.} 3.Nc3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the knight just over 20% of the time. In most positions like this, we will push the pawn to e5 to grab some more space and attack the knight. This makes our position easier to play and forces Black to rely on flank pawn breaks like ...c5.} 4.e5 4...Nfd7 {Black usually goes to d7. I now prefer d4, connecting the pawns.} 5.d4 5...c5 {Black plays ...c5, undermining our structure. This is the key move they have to know. I actually recommend capturing on c5 because we don't have time to defend the d4-pawn with another pawn.} 6.dxc5 6...Bxc5 {Black recaptures. Our bishop is well placed on the b1-h7 diagonal when the pawn is on e5.} 7.Bd3 7...Nc6 {Black develops, attacking e5. And we can develop, defending.} 8.Bf4 8...a6 {Black sometimes goes for ...a6, thinking of ...b5. This is another approach they can take. We'll just castle and let them play it.} 9.O-O 9...b5 {And if b5...} 10.Re1 {We should overprotect e5. White is doing well here.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/80444ee0-58da-4520-9133-65cf4805bdc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black goes for the French 10% of the time, which is still almost 380 million games in the database! The pawn on e6 supports the d5-advancement, but blocks in the bishop on c8. We'll simply develop our knight for now.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And once Black goes for d5... We bring our other knight out. This is our starting position with the two knights on f3 and c3. In this study, we'll analyze the 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 responses.} 3.Nc3 3...d4 {Black pushes ...d4 just over a quarter of the time. This move, attacking the knight, is the second most popular move in the database. I suggest we bring our knight back, putting pressure on d4.} 4.Ne2 4...c5 {And by far the most popular continuation is ...c5, claiming that central space. It's best to immediately challenge the structure. The move ...d3 isn't possible because the pawn is too far advanced and it can't be defended after Nf4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {...Nc6 is the most popular move. Let's capture once.} 6.cxd4 6...cxd4 {Black recaptures. And now we can pin the knight, threatening to win the pawn.} 7.Qa4 7...Bd7 {The bishop can come to d7 and unpin. However, that gives us a pawn as we can just take on d4. After this the computer recommends ...Nge7 for Black, but the most common move is certainly ...Nxd4.} 8.Nexd4 8...Nxd4 {Our queen is under attack now. So we will recapture with it.} 9.Qxd4 9...Nf6 {And after the knight is developed...} 10.Be2 {It's time to consolidate being a pawn up. We'll play standard moves like d3 and O-O, and we can activate our dark-squared bishop via Bd2-Bc3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/80444ee0-58da-4520-9133-65cf4805bdc2/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black goes for the French 10% of the time, which is still almost 380 million games in the database! The pawn on e6 supports the d5-advancement, but blocks in the bishop on c8. We'll simply develop our knight for now.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And once Black goes for d5... We bring our other knight out. This is our starting position with the two knights on f3 and c3. In this study, we'll analyze the 3...Nf6 and 3...d4 responses.} 3.Nc3 3...d4 {Black pushes ...d4 just over a quarter of the time. This move, attacking the knight, is the second most popular move in the database. I suggest we bring our knight back, putting pressure on d4.} 4.Ne2 4...c5 {And by far the most popular continuation is ...c5, claiming that central space. It's best to immediately challenge the structure. The move ...d3 isn't possible because the pawn is too far advanced and it can't be defended after Nf4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {...Nc6 is the most popular move. Let's capture once.} 6.cxd4 6...cxd4 {Black recaptures. And now we can pin the knight, threatening to win the pawn.} 7.Qa4 7...Bd7 {The bishop can come to d7 and unpin. However, that gives us a pawn as we can just take on d4. After this the computer recommends ...Nge7 for Black, but the most common move is certainly ...Nxd4.} 8.Nexd4 8...Ne5 {This discovered attack is very popular. But we can just develop and block the attack. The point of this move is that we'll just trade Black's attackers.} 9.Bb5 9...Nd3+ {Don't be afraid of this check. We can chase the knight away.} 10.Ke2 10...Nf4+ {And if Black checks you again...} 11.Kf1 {Your king will be safe enough and you'll be a pawn up. Black will try to develop with moves like ...Nf6, so you can continue with moves like Bxd7+, to get pieces off the board, followed by g3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...dxe4 and 3...c5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/1bacf548-ac67-4ce1-81ca-04fcc5c32c78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black prepares the French. We get our kingside knight out.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. And we activate both knights in response.} 3.Nc3 3...c5 {3...c5, gaining space, is also played 10% of the time, but it's just bad. Black is simply too far down in development and is focusing on pawn play more than they should be. We'll capture on d5 first.} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Black recaptures, and you'll notice the pawns are potential targets. So we'll develop quickly first. The check on b5 brings the bishop out and attacks the king. We're clearing the way for castling.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Nc6 {The knight blocks. And we'll castle.} 6.O-O 6...d4 {Some players may try to get aggressive with their central pawns. But recall that after castling, our rook can come into the game. This check is hard to deal with. There are many tactics having to do with Ne4 and Ne5 depending on what Black plays.} 7.Re1+ 7...Be7 {This block is by far the most popular. Ne4 now attacks the c5-pawn.} 8.Ne4 8...Qb6 {Black tries to get the queen in.} 9.Nd6+ {But doing so loses castling rights. After ...Kf8 we can take the knight on c6, followed by Nc4 and d3 to develop the dark-squared bishop. Black will have trouble with their kingside coordination because of their uncastled king.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...dxe4 and 3...c5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/1bacf548-ac67-4ce1-81ca-04fcc5c32c78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black prepares the French. We get our kingside knight out.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. And we activate both knights in response.} 3.Nc3 3...dxe4 {Black makes the trade on e4 over 30% of the time, making it the most popular move. Black wants to get rid of our central pawn and target our knights in the center. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxe4 4...Nf6 {This is the most popular move at this point. But it's not the most accurate. We will take.} 5.Nxf6+ 5...Qxf6 {And once the queen recaptures... We threaten Bg5, attacking the queen.} 6.d4 6...h6 {That's why ...h6 is the most popular move. We can simply continue developing at this point.} 7.Bd3 7...Bd6 {Black develops as well. And White is better after Qe2. We have stronger central control and more active pieces.} 8.Qe2 8...Nc6 {Black might go for ...Nc6, trying to attack our d4-pawn. But c3 is a simple defense.} 9.c3 9...O-O {Castling now is a bit different, since if Qe4, Black has ...Qf5.} 10.h4 {But we can go for an attack instead! The move h4 begins the pawn storm. We'll continue with Be3, O-O-O, and try to unleash even more forces against the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...dxe4 and 3...c5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/1bacf548-ac67-4ce1-81ca-04fcc5c32c78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black prepares the French. We get our kingside knight out.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. And we activate both knights in response.} 3.Nc3 3...c5 {3...c5, gaining space, is also played 10% of the time, but it's just bad. Black is simply too far down in development and is focusing on pawn play more than they should be. We'll capture on d5 first.} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Black recaptures, and you'll notice the pawns are potential targets. So we'll develop quickly first. The check on b5 brings the bishop out and attacks the king. We're clearing the way for castling.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Nc6 {The knight blocks. And we'll castle.} 6.O-O 6...Nf6 {Black usually develops the knight. We have room to strike in the center now.} 7.d4 7...Be7 {And now even the very popular ...Be7 is inaccurate. Ne5 puts more pressure on c6.} 8.Ne5 8...Bd7 {When Black defends... We can take the bishop on d7.} 9.Nxd7 9...Qxd7 {The queen enters a pin...} 10.dxc5 {And now we make Black's position really awkward, since ...Bxc5 allows Re1+. Black will most likely castle. So we can continue with moves like Be3-Bd4, and even b4 to hang on to the pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...dxe4 and 3...c5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/1bacf548-ac67-4ce1-81ca-04fcc5c32c78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black prepares the French. We get our kingside knight out.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. And we activate both knights in response.} 3.Nc3 3...dxe4 {Black makes the trade on e4 over 30% of the time, making it the most popular move. Black wants to get rid of our central pawn and target our knights in the center. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxe4 4...Nd7 {...Nd7 is necessary before ...Ngf6 to support the knight. We'll advance in the center anyway.} 5.d4 5...Ngf6 {Once the knight comes to f6, we don't really have a reason to retreat. We'll trade.} 6.Nxf6+ 6...Nxf6 {Black recaptures. The d3-square is best for our bishop.} 7.Bd3 7...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop.} 8.O-O {And both sides will calmly castle. White can continue with Qe2, Bf4 and c3. Black has a problem piece: the bishop on c8. Luckily, White won't have any problems to develop. A strong grip on the e5-square will prevent any ...e5 pushes.} 8...O-O {White can continue with Qe2, Bf4 and c3. Black has a problem piece: the bishop on c8. Luckily White won't have any problems to develop. A strong grip on the e5-square will prevent any ...e5 pushes.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...dxe4 and 3...c5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/1bacf548-ac67-4ce1-81ca-04fcc5c32c78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black prepares the French. We get our kingside knight out.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. And we activate both knights in response.} 3.Nc3 3...dxe4 {Black makes the trade on e4 over 30% of the time, making it the most popular move. Black wants to get rid of our central pawn and target our knights in the center. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxe4 4...Nf6 {This is the most popular move at this point. But it's not the most accurate. We will take.} 5.Nxf6+ 5...gxf6 {Some players like recapturing with the g-pawn because the doubled pawns are actually not weak. They can control many squares in the center, and the rook also gains a semi-open g-file. We'll play d4, controlling the center and opening up our bishop.} 6.d4 6...Nc6 {What you'll often see is Black developing to c6. Our bishop can come and overprotect d4.} 7.Be3 7...b6 {Since the doubled pawns prevent the light-squared bishop's development, what you'll often see is Black going for ...b6 and ...Bb7. I suggest just going for natural development here. We want to castle long since the g-file is open.} 8.Qd2 8...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes...} 9.O-O-O {And we'll castle queenside. We'll continue with moves like Bc4 and Kb1. Rhe1 is also on the horizon. Our strategy consists of superior central control and keeping a better pawn structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...dxe4 and 3...c5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/1bacf548-ac67-4ce1-81ca-04fcc5c32c78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black prepares the French. We get our kingside knight out.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. And we activate both knights in response.} 3.Nc3 3...c5 {3...c5, gaining space, is also played 10% of the time, but it's just bad. Black is simply too far down in development and is focusing on pawn play more than they should be. We'll capture on d5 first.} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Black recaptures, and you'll notice the pawns are potential targets. So we'll develop quickly first. The check on b5 brings the bishop out and attacks the king. We're clearing the way for castling.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Bd7 {Blocking with the bishop is a blunder. Many people fall for this. You'll notice that the bishop on d7 disconnects the queen's defense of d4. We'll give a check on e2, defending the bishop first.} 6.Qe2+ 6...Be7 {Black blocks.} 7.Nxd5 {And we're a pawn up. If ...Bxb5, Qxb5+ and Black is in more trouble: ...Nc6 and the b7-pawn falls. It's now time to consolidate. So if Black just develops with ...Nc6, b3 for Bb2 and O-O are good options for White.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...dxe4 and 3...c5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/1bacf548-ac67-4ce1-81ca-04fcc5c32c78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black prepares the French. We get our kingside knight out.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. And we activate both knights in response.} 3.Nc3 3...dxe4 {Black makes the trade on e4 over 30% of the time, making it the most popular move. Black wants to get rid of our central pawn and target our knights in the center. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxe4 4...Nf6 {This is the most popular move at this point. But it's not the most accurate. We will take.} 5.Nxf6+ 5...Qxf6 {And once the queen recaptures... We threaten Bg5, attacking the queen.} 6.d4 6...h6 {That's why ...h6 is the most popular move. We can simply continue developing at this point.} 7.Bd3 7...Bd6 {Black develops as well. And White is better after Qe2. We have stronger central control and more active pieces.} 8.Qe2 8...c5 {If you see Black play ...c5 at this point, you can just capture. This forces Black's bishop to move once again and Black hasn't achieved much.} 9.dxc5 9...Bxc5 {When Black recaptures...} 10.O-O {We can continue with sound, developing moves like Re1, c3 and Be3. Sure, Black has removed our central pawn, but we'll get a lot of activity in exchange for that. We'll centralize both rooks, bringing them to the open and semi-open d- and e-files, and enjoy more space for our pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...dxe4 and 3...c5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/1bacf548-ac67-4ce1-81ca-04fcc5c32c78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black prepares the French. We get our kingside knight out.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. And we activate both knights in response.} 3.Nc3 3...dxe4 {Black makes the trade on e4 over 30% of the time, making it the most popular move. Black wants to get rid of our central pawn and target our knights in the center. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxe4 4...Nf6 {This is the most popular move at this point. But it's not the most accurate. We will take.} 5.Nxf6+ 5...Qxf6 {And once the queen recaptures... We threaten Bg5, attacking the queen.} 6.d4 6...h6 {That's why ...h6 is the most popular move. We can simply continue developing at this point.} 7.Bd3 7...Bd6 {Black develops as well. And White is better after Qe2. We have stronger central control and more active pieces.} 8.Qe2 8...O-O {For example, castling, believe it or not, is a very popular blunder here.} 9.Qe4 {We have Qe4, threatening checkmate! And now Black must lose a pawn to prevent checkmate or simply acquire a worse position. If ...Qf5, we have Qh4 with a discovered attack and Bxh6 sacrifices in the air!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The French Defense / Study 2: Black Plays 3...dxe4 and 3...c5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ae0408df-3377-48f4-b592-c2bdf54bb088/studies/1bacf548-ac67-4ce1-81ca-04fcc5c32c78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e6 {Black prepares the French. We get our kingside knight out.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. And we activate both knights in response.} 3.Nc3 3...dxe4 {Black makes the trade on e4 over 30% of the time, making it the most popular move. Black wants to get rid of our central pawn and target our knights in the center. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxe4 4...Nf6 {This is the most popular move at this point. But it's not the most accurate. We will take.} 5.Nxf6+ 5...Qxf6 {And once the queen recaptures... We threaten Bg5, attacking the queen.} 6.d4 6...Bd6 {If Black just develops their dark-squared bishop... We won't play Bg5 right away. Rather, we'll set up a trap.} 7.Bd3 7...O-O {Should Black castle...} 8.Bg5 {The queen is trapped. Good game!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Bf5 and 4...h5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/32af33de-67b3-4f34-9f1d-c8003b3da086/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. It's chosen 7% of the time, with more than 260 million games in the database. The idea is simple: The move ...c6 supports the advance with ...d5 without blocking in the c8-bishop. If allowed, we should place two pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn. We grab some space and close the center by pushing the pawn to e5. This is the Advance Variation, and it's one of the best weapons against the Caro, at all levels.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5 65% of the time, which is one of the two main moves. The idea behind this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain before Black plays ...e6. I'm going to recommend h4, which in my opinion is the most critical and challenging way to fight Black's setup.} 4.h4 4...h5 {Black responds with ...h5 43% of the time, freeing up the h7-square and preventing our h-pawn from advancing any further. This is a direct way of preventing us from trapping their bishop. We'll develop our dark-squared bishop to g5 in order to pin Black's e-pawn, since they want to play ...e6 now.} 5.Bg5 5...Qb6 {Black moves the queen to b6 and attacks the weak b2-pawn. However, there is actually no need for us to defend the b2-pawn. Instead, we can simply respond by developing our light-squared bishop to d3 and offering a trade. This even leaves the d4-pawn hanging, too!} 6.Bd3 6...Bxd3 {Most often, Black opts to exchange the light-squared bishops on d3. We'll recapture with the queen.} 7.Qxd3 7...Qxb2 {Black will probably capture on b2 at some point. This is already a common motif: we ignore the attack on our rook by pushing the e-pawn up to e6! If Black does nothing, we'll capture the f7-pawn and expose Black's king.} 8.e6 8...Qxa1 {Black captures the rook and threatens our knight and the a2-pawn. We'll move our queen over to b3. This move defends the knight and pawn, but it also takes away some squares from Black's queen and attacks the b7-pawn.} 9.Qb3 9...Qxd4 {Black captures the pawn on d4. This is another option.} 10.Qxb7 {We capture the pawn on b7. Not only does this win Black's rook, but it also threatens a checkmate with Qc8#. Those threats, in conjunction with the useful retreating move Be3, secure White a winning advantage!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Bf5 and 4...h5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/32af33de-67b3-4f34-9f1d-c8003b3da086/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. It's chosen 7% of the time, with more than 260 million games in the database. The idea is simple: The move ...c6 supports the advance with ...d5 without blocking in the c8-bishop. If allowed, we should place two pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn. We grab some space and close the center by pushing the pawn to e5. This is the Advance Variation, and it's one of the best weapons against the Caro, at all levels.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5 65% of the time, which is one of the two main moves. The idea behind this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain before Black plays ...e6. I'm going to recommend h4, which in my opinion is the most critical and challenging way to fight Black's setup.} 4.h4 4...h5 {Black responds with ...h5 43% of the time, freeing up the h7-square and preventing our h-pawn from advancing any further. This is a direct way of preventing us from trapping their bishop. We'll develop our dark-squared bishop to g5 in order to pin Black's e-pawn, since they want to play ...e6 now.} 5.Bg5 5...Qb6 {Black moves the queen to b6 and attacks the weak b2-pawn. However, there is actually no need for us to defend the b2-pawn. Instead, we can simply respond by developing our light-squared bishop to d3 and offering a trade. This even leaves the d4-pawn hanging, too!} 6.Bd3 6...Bxd3 {Most often, Black opts to exchange the light-squared bishops on d3. We'll recapture with the queen.} 7.Qxd3 7...Qxb2 {Black will probably capture on b2 at some point. This is already a common motif: we ignore the attack on our rook by pushing the e-pawn up to e6! If Black does nothing, we'll capture the f7-pawn and expose Black's king.} 8.e6 8...Qxa1 {Black captures the rook and threatens our knight and the a2-pawn. We'll move our queen over to b3. This move defends the knight and pawn, but it also takes away some squares from Black's queen and attacks the b7-pawn.} 9.Qb3 9...b6 {Black goes for ...b6 to defend. We'll activate the knight to e2, defending the d4-pawn. We'll castle next move.} 10.Ne2 10...fxe6 {Black captures the pawn on e6.} 11.O-O {But once we castle, Black is lost! Our whole idea was to trap Black's queen and castle as quick as possible. Now once we move the knight from b1, Black won't be able to save the queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Bf5 and 4...h5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/32af33de-67b3-4f34-9f1d-c8003b3da086/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. It's chosen 7% of the time, with more than 260 million games in the database. The idea is simple: The move ...c6 supports the advance with ...d5 without blocking in the c8-bishop. If allowed, we should place two pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn. We grab some space and close the center by pushing the pawn to e5. This is the Advance Variation, and it's one of the best weapons against the Caro, at all levels.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5 65% of the time, which is one of the two main moves. The idea behind this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain before Black plays ...e6. I'm going to recommend h4, which in my opinion is the most critical and challenging way to fight Black's setup.} 4.h4 4...h5 {Black responds with ...h5 43% of the time, freeing up the h7-square and preventing our h-pawn from advancing any further. This is a direct way of preventing us from trapping their bishop. We'll develop our dark-squared bishop to g5 in order to pin Black's e-pawn, since they want to play ...e6 now.} 5.Bg5 5...Qb6 {Black moves the queen to b6 and attacks the weak b2-pawn. However, there is actually no need for us to defend the b2-pawn. Instead, we can simply respond by developing our light-squared bishop to d3 and offering a trade. This even leaves the d4-pawn hanging, too!} 6.Bd3 6...Bxd3 {Most often, Black opts to exchange the light-squared bishops on d3. We'll recapture with the queen.} 7.Qxd3 7...Qxb2 {Black will probably capture on b2 at some point. This is already a common motif: we ignore the attack on our rook by pushing the e-pawn up to e6! If Black does nothing, we'll capture the f7-pawn and expose Black's king.} 8.e6 8...Qxa1 {Black captures the rook and threatens our knight and the a2-pawn. We'll move our queen over to b3. This move defends the knight and pawn, but it also takes away some squares from Black's queen and attacks the b7-pawn.} 9.Qb3 9...b5 {Black defends against Qxb7 by pushing the b-pawn. A natural move is to develop our kingside knight to e2. This move defends the d4-pawn and prepares kingside castling.} 10.Ne2 10...a5 {Black continues expanding on the queenside. Once they capture our rook and their queen is stuck, this is the standard approach. We simply castle our king.} 11.O-O 11...a4 {Black pushes the a-pawn forward and attacks our queen.} 12.Qa3 {We simply slide the queen over to a3. We'll continue with Nd2 (or Nc3), and Black is simply lost as there is no way to save the queen on a1!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Bf5 and 4...h5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/32af33de-67b3-4f34-9f1d-c8003b3da086/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. It's chosen 7% of the time, with more than 260 million games in the database. The idea is simple: The move ...c6 supports the advance with ...d5 without blocking in the c8-bishop. If allowed, we should place two pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn. We grab some space and close the center by pushing the pawn to e5. This is the Advance Variation, and it's one of the best weapons against the Caro, at all levels.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5 65% of the time, which is one of the two main moves. The idea behind this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain before Black plays ...e6. I'm going to recommend h4, which in my opinion is the most critical and challenging way to fight Black's setup.} 4.h4 4...h5 {Black responds with ...h5 43% of the time, freeing up the h7-square and preventing our h-pawn from advancing any further. This is a direct way of preventing us from trapping their bishop. We'll develop our dark-squared bishop to g5 in order to pin Black's e-pawn, since they want to play ...e6 now.} 5.Bg5 5...Qb6 {Black moves the queen to b6 and attacks the weak b2-pawn. However, there is actually no need for us to defend the b2-pawn. Instead, we can simply respond by developing our light-squared bishop to d3 and offering a trade. This even leaves the d4-pawn hanging, too!} 6.Bd3 6...Bxd3 {Most often, Black opts to exchange the light-squared bishops on d3. We'll recapture with the queen.} 7.Qxd3 7...e6 {Black doesn't have to take on b2. They can continue with ...e6, too.} 8.Nd2 {We can simply develop our knight to d2. White has a pleasant edge and can look to continue with Nf3, c4 and O-O. ...Qxb2 can always be met with Rb1.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Bf5 and 4...h5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/32af33de-67b3-4f34-9f1d-c8003b3da086/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. It's chosen 7% of the time, with more than 260 million games in the database. The idea is simple: The move ...c6 supports the advance with ...d5 without blocking in the c8-bishop. If allowed, we should place two pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn. We grab some space and close the center by pushing the pawn to e5. This is the Advance Variation, and it's one of the best weapons against the Caro, at all levels.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5 65% of the time, which is one of the two main moves. The idea behind this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain before Black plays ...e6. I'm going to recommend h4, which in my opinion is the most critical and challenging way to fight Black's setup.} 4.h4 4...h5 {Black responds with ...h5 43% of the time, freeing up the h7-square and preventing our h-pawn from advancing any further. This is a direct way of preventing us from trapping their bishop. We'll develop our dark-squared bishop to g5 in order to pin Black's e-pawn, since they want to play ...e6 now.} 5.Bg5 5...Qb6 {Black moves the queen to b6 and attacks the weak b2-pawn. However, there is actually no need for us to defend the b2-pawn. Instead, we can simply respond by developing our light-squared bishop to d3 and offering a trade. This even leaves the d4-pawn hanging, too!} 6.Bd3 6...Qxd4 {The critical response is Black capturing on d4. We'll develop the knight with tempo on the queen and await Black's response.} 7.Nf3 7...Qg4 {Black moves the queen over to g4 and defends the bishop. Let's trade anyway.} 8.Bxf5 8...Qxf5 {Black recaptures on f5.} 9.O-O {We simply castle our king. Black is now up a pawn, but White has good compensation due to the lead in development and superior piece activity. A model game from this position is Barbosa - Molina, 2013. Here's the link to the game: https://lichess.org/UXV4k96t#17} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Bf5 and 4...h5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/32af33de-67b3-4f34-9f1d-c8003b3da086/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. It's chosen 7% of the time, with more than 260 million games in the database. The idea is simple: The move ...c6 supports the advance with ...d5 without blocking in the c8-bishop. If allowed, we should place two pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn. We grab some space and close the center by pushing the pawn to e5. This is the Advance Variation, and it's one of the best weapons against the Caro, at all levels.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5 65% of the time, which is one of the two main moves. The idea behind this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain before Black plays ...e6. I'm going to recommend h4, which in my opinion is the most critical and challenging way to fight Black's setup.} 4.h4 4...h5 {Black responds with ...h5 43% of the time, freeing up the h7-square and preventing our h-pawn from advancing any further. This is a direct way of preventing us from trapping their bishop. We'll develop our dark-squared bishop to g5 in order to pin Black's e-pawn, since they want to play ...e6 now.} 5.Bg5 5...Qb6 {Black moves the queen to b6 and attacks the weak b2-pawn. However, there is actually no need for us to defend the b2-pawn. Instead, we can simply respond by developing our light-squared bishop to d3 and offering a trade. This even leaves the d4-pawn hanging, too!} 6.Bd3 6...Qxd4 {The critical response is Black capturing on d4. We'll develop the knight with tempo on the queen and await Black's response.} 7.Nf3 7...Qxb2 {Black could opt to take on b2. We'll capture the bishop in that case.} 8.Bxf5 8...Qxa1 {Black captures the rook on a1.} 9.e6 {We get a similar position to the 6...Qxb2 variation. We are better here since the e6-pawn creates a lot of problems for Black, together with their queen on a1 which is out of bounds.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Bf5 and 4...h5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/32af33de-67b3-4f34-9f1d-c8003b3da086/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. It's chosen 7% of the time, with more than 260 million games in the database. The idea is simple: The move ...c6 supports the advance with ...d5 without blocking in the c8-bishop. If allowed, we should place two pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn. We grab some space and close the center by pushing the pawn to e5. This is the Advance Variation, and it's one of the best weapons against the Caro, at all levels.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5 65% of the time, which is one of the two main moves. The idea behind this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain before Black plays ...e6. I'm going to recommend h4, which in my opinion is the most critical and challenging way to fight Black's setup.} 4.h4 4...h5 {Black responds with ...h5 43% of the time, freeing up the h7-square and preventing our h-pawn from advancing any further. This is a direct way of preventing us from trapping their bishop. We'll develop our dark-squared bishop to g5 in order to pin Black's e-pawn, since they want to play ...e6 now.} 5.Bg5 5...Qb6 {Black moves the queen to b6 and attacks the weak b2-pawn. However, there is actually no need for us to defend the b2-pawn. Instead, we can simply respond by developing our light-squared bishop to d3 and offering a trade. This even leaves the d4-pawn hanging, too!} 6.Bd3 6...Qxb2 {Black captures the pawn on b2. This is a mistake. We capture the bishop on f5.} 7.Bxf5 7...Qxa1 {Black takes the rook.} 8.e6 {But we push e6 with an advantage! We are down an exchange, but Black is really cramped and underdeveloped. Black can move the f-pawn, evading the tension but cramping their pieces even more. Otherwise, we will capture on f7 and expose Black's king even more.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...Bf5 and 4...h5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/32af33de-67b3-4f34-9f1d-c8003b3da086/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. It's chosen 7% of the time, with more than 260 million games in the database. The idea is simple: The move ...c6 supports the advance with ...d5 without blocking in the c8-bishop. If allowed, we should place two pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn. We grab some space and close the center by pushing the pawn to e5. This is the Advance Variation, and it's one of the best weapons against the Caro, at all levels.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5 65% of the time, which is one of the two main moves. The idea behind this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain before Black plays ...e6. I'm going to recommend h4, which in my opinion is the most critical and challenging way to fight Black's setup.} 4.h4 4...e6 {The move ...e6 is simply a blunder, and it's played almost 20% of the time! This is a good example of why the aggressive h4 can be so effective. We'll follow up with g4 to attack the bishop.} 5.g4 5...Be4 {Black moves the bishop to e4. We'll continue attacking it with f3, naturally.} 6.f3 6...Bg6 {Black retreats the bishop to g6.} 7.h5 {We trap Black's bishop with h5 and Black has no way to save it! This is one of the ideas behind h4. Black must not play ...e6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...h6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/c9ff7ec9-794e-4cee-8db4-e9ef60af1cd4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. We expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing to ...d5. We'll play e5.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5. The main idea of this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain. Let's go for h4 and consider Black's options.} 4.h4 4...h6 {Black responds with ...h6, freeing up the h7-square for their bishop. This move occurs just over 30% of the time. We'll use the opportunity to continue with g4.} 5.g4 5...Bh7 {Black retreats the bishop to h7, which is the most common. We'll go for our e6-idea, which we saw in the 5...Be4 branch.} 6.e6 6...fxe6 {Black accepts the sacrifice by capturing the pawn on e6. Let's offer a trade of light-squared bishops.} 7.Bd3 7...Nf6 {Black doesn't trade on d3 and instead develops the kingside knight to f6.} 8.Bxh7 {We trade bishops on h7. White is better, and our ideas are pretty much all the same: Qd3 and Nf3-Ne5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...h6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/c9ff7ec9-794e-4cee-8db4-e9ef60af1cd4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. We expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing to ...d5. We'll play e5.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5. The main idea of this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain. Let's go for h4 and consider Black's options.} 4.h4 4...h6 {Black responds with ...h6, freeing up the h7-square for their bishop. This move occurs just over 30% of the time. We'll use the opportunity to continue with g4.} 5.g4 5...Bd7 {Black retreats the bishop to d7. This is the main move, but not everybody might know that. Black accepts a passive position and tries to fight the massive pawn expansion. We'll proceed by developing our queenside knight to c3.} 6.Nc3 6...e6 {Black leaves the bishop behind the pawn chain with ...e6. We'll develop our dark-squared bishop to e3, supporting the d4-pawn.} 7.Be3 7...Qb6 {The queen comes out to b6, attacking the b2-pawn. Since our knight is already on c3, we can jump to a4 and both attack the queen and defend the pawn.} 8.Na4 8...Qa5+ {Black moves the queen out to a5 and attacks our king and knight. We'll play c3, blocking the check and defending the knight with our queen.} 9.c3 9...b5 {Black attacks the knight with ...b5. We'll jump to the only safe square.} 10.Nc5 10...Bxc5 {Our knight is pretty annoying on c5, so Black trades the bishop for it. We recapture on c5. Black has to act quickly or b4 is coming.} 11.dxc5 11...b4 {Black prevents b4. We'll attack the structure.} 12.a3 12...bxc3 {Black captures the pawn on c3. But faces the surprising b4.} 13.b4 13...Qc7 {Black retreats the queen to c7 and attacks the e5-pawn.} 14.Bd4 {This is a pretty similar position to one we got in one of the previous lines, except we are placing the bishop on d4 now. The idea is simple, we will capture on c3 and we've got the advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...h6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/c9ff7ec9-794e-4cee-8db4-e9ef60af1cd4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. We expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing to ...d5. We'll play e5.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5. The main idea of this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain. Let's go for h4 and consider Black's options.} 4.h4 4...h6 {Black responds with ...h6, freeing up the h7-square for their bishop. This move occurs just over 30% of the time. We'll use the opportunity to continue with g4.} 5.g4 5...Bd7 {Black retreats the bishop to d7. This is the main move, but not everybody might know that. Black accepts a passive position and tries to fight the massive pawn expansion. We'll proceed by developing our queenside knight to c3.} 6.Nc3 6...Qc8 {Black slides the queen over to c8 and lines up an attack on the g4-pawn.} 7.Be2 {We'll defend the pawn with Be2. White now has a pleasant edge, and we'll continue with Be3 and Nf3.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...h6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/c9ff7ec9-794e-4cee-8db4-e9ef60af1cd4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. We expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing to ...d5. We'll play e5.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5. The main idea of this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain. Let's go for h4 and consider Black's options.} 4.h4 4...h6 {Black responds with ...h6, freeing up the h7-square for their bishop. This move occurs just over 30% of the time. We'll use the opportunity to continue with g4.} 5.g4 5...Be4 {Sometimes Black moves the bishop to e4 in order to induce f3 before going back to h7. We have to play f3.} 6.f3 6...Bh7 {Black retreats the bishop to the only safe square, h7. And here we implement an important positional sacrifice: e6. We want to disrupt Black's coordination and pawn structure as much as possible and slow down their development.} 7.e6 7...fxe6 {Black accepts the sacrifice by capturing the pawn on e6. Let's try to capitalize on the light squares by trading the bishops first.} 8.Bd3 8...Bxd3 {Black obliges and trades on d3.} 9.Qxd3 {We recapture with the queen. If Black now develops the kingside knight, we'll continue with Ne2 and Bf4.} 9...Qd6 {Black moves the queen up to d6 instead.} 10.f4 {We'll implement the f4-idea, looking to play Nf3. White is now much better. For the cost of a pawn, we have more space, dominant control over the light squares and a great grip over the e5-square.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...h6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/c9ff7ec9-794e-4cee-8db4-e9ef60af1cd4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. We expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing to ...d5. We'll play e5.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5. The main idea of this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain. Let's go for h4 and consider Black's options.} 4.h4 4...h6 {Black responds with ...h6, freeing up the h7-square for their bishop. This move occurs just over 30% of the time. We'll use the opportunity to continue with g4.} 5.g4 5...Bh7 {Black retreats the bishop to h7, which is the most common. We'll go for our e6-idea, which we saw in the 5...Be4 branch.} 6.e6 6...fxe6 {Black accepts the sacrifice by capturing the pawn on e6. Let's offer a trade of light-squared bishops.} 7.Bd3 7...Bxd3 {Black obliges and trades on d3.} 8.Qxd3 {We recapture with the queen. White is now simply better and, for the cost of one pawn, has dominant control over the light squares. Some typical ideas are Nf3-Ne5, and the obvious Qg6+.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...h6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/c9ff7ec9-794e-4cee-8db4-e9ef60af1cd4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. We expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing to ...d5. We'll play e5.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5. The main idea of this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain. Let's go for h4 and consider Black's options.} 4.h4 4...h6 {Black responds with ...h6, freeing up the h7-square for their bishop. This move occurs just over 30% of the time. We'll use the opportunity to continue with g4.} 5.g4 5...Be4 {Sometimes Black moves the bishop to e4 in order to induce f3 before going back to h7. We have to play f3.} 6.f3 6...Bh7 {Black retreats the bishop to the only safe square, h7. And here we implement an important positional sacrifice: e6. We want to disrupt Black's coordination and pawn structure as much as possible and slow down their development.} 7.e6 7...Qd6 {Black declines the sacrifice and attacks the pawn with their queen. They might want to come to g3. We'll capture with check in that case.} 8.exf7+ 8...Kxf7 {Black recaptures on f7. We' ll push the pawn to f4, gaining some space and looking to develop our knight behind it. This takes even more control of the e5-square.} 9.f4 9...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight to f6 and attacks the g4-pawn, which is now weaker. It seems like we can't play Nf3 now because of the threat on g4, but that is exactly what we'll do! The knight on f3 will give us many crucial prospects in the position, combined with the next move we'll play.} 10.Nf3 10...Nxg4 {Black grabs the pawn on g4.} 11.h5 {We push our h-pawn to h5, further cramping Black's position. With the space advantage and great attacking prospects on the kingside, White has sufficient compensation for the pawn. We'll look to continue with Bh3 and Ne5. Black's pieces are all over the place.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...h6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/c9ff7ec9-794e-4cee-8db4-e9ef60af1cd4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. We expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing to ...d5. We'll play e5.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5. The main idea of this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain. Let's go for h4 and consider Black's options.} 4.h4 4...h6 {Black responds with ...h6, freeing up the h7-square for their bishop. This move occurs just over 30% of the time. We'll use the opportunity to continue with g4.} 5.g4 5...Bh7 {Black retreats the bishop to h7, which is the most common. We'll go for our e6-idea, which we saw in the 5...Be4 branch.} 6.e6 6...Qd6 {Black declines the sacrifice and attacks the pawn with their queen. We'll take on f7.} 7.exf7+ 7...Kxf7 {Black recaptures on f7.} 8.g5 {We push our g-pawn to g5. White now has a convincing edge. Black's king is exposed and our attacking possibilites are tremendous. We can continue with Nf3 and Bh3.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Caro-Kann Part 1 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...h6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/6da17992-a49d-4844-8fa8-bacf3a625cc8/studies/c9ff7ec9-794e-4cee-8db4-e9ef60af1cd4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black replies with 1...c6. We expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing to ...d5. We'll play e5.} 3.e5 3...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5. The main idea of this move is to develop the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain. Let's go for h4 and consider Black's options.} 4.h4 4...Qb6 {Black moves the queen to b6 only 1% of the time. The idea is that Black is preventing us from easily developing the dark-squared bishop. If Black hasn't stopped us from doing so, we'll attack the bishop with g4.} 5.g4 5...Bd7 {Black retreats the bishop to d7. Normal development is fine here. Our knight could pose some problems to Black's queen soon.} 6.Nc3 6...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop, "kills" the light-squared bishop, and sets up a solid light-squared pawn chain with ...e6. In response to this passive and slow setup, we will jump to a4 and attack Black's queen! This wins a tempo and forces Black to move the queen again.} 7.Na4 7...Qa5+ {Black moves the queen out to a5 and gives a check. We'll play c3, blocking the check and defending the knight with our queen. If Black now plays ...c5, we should capture it with our knight.} 8.c3 8...b5 {Black goes for ...b5 instead, attacking the knight once again. The only safe square for the knight is c5.} 9.Nc5 9...Bxc5 {Our knight is pretty annoying on c5, so Black trades the bishop for it. We recapture on c5. If Black now doesn't act quickly, we'll bolster the c5-pawn with b4.} 10.dxc5 10...b4 {Black pushes their b-pawn forward and prevents b4. Instead of capturing on b4, we'll push a3 and challenge Black's structure. This serves a hidden purpose which you will see on the next move.} 11.a3 11...bxc3 {Black captures the pawn on c3, but we were prepared for this. We suddenly push b4 and attack the Black queen! This connects our pawns as well.} 12.b4 12...Qc7 {Black retreats the queen to c7 and attacks the e5-pawn.} 13.Qd4 {We'll continue with Qd4, looking to capture the pawn on c3 at some point. White's position is simply amazing. We have a massive space advantage on all three parts of the board, and Black's light-squared bishop is inactive.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Caro-Kann Part 2 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...c5 and 4...Nc6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/25e3f0cf-0b90-49b1-862f-f5c5cf625314/studies/606388b1-3bc2-4ed2-96d4-59df6d4e9f81/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black goes for the Caro-Kann. We'll place both pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn. We grab some space and close the center by pushing the pawn to e5. The Advance Variation.} 3.e5 3...c5 {Black strikes in the center with ...c5 just over 20% of the time, challenging our d4-pawn. They lose a tempo, but at the same time hope we play c3 so that they achieve a French-like structure without their bishop being blocked in. But the better move is to capture on c5!} 4.dxc5 4...Nc6 {Black opts for ...Nc6 in this study, attacking our e5-pawn. Expect to see this more than 65% of the time. Once we capture on c5 and accept the sacrifice, our goal is to be as annoying as possible and try to hold on to our pawns while developing. That's why we will defend the e5-pawn with f4!} 5.f4 5...e6 {Most often does Black try to go after our c5-pawn. We develop the dark-squared bishop to e3 and support the c5-pawn. ...Qa5+ doesn't really work because of c3 with b4 ideas.} 6.Be3 6...d4 {Things might seem dangerous if Black pushes the d-pawn to d4, attacking our bishop and interfering with the defense of the c5-pawn. We'll calmly slide our bishop to f2 and allow Black to take on c5.} 7.Bf2 7...Bxc5 {Black captures the c5-pawn. They have gotten their pawn back, but have extended the d4-pawn in the process and weakened the e4-square. We develop our knight to d2, eyeing e4 and preparing to castle queenside in the future.} 8.Nd2 8...Nge7 {Black develops the knight to e7, preparing ...O-O and looking to jump the knight to d5 or f5. Before anything else, we'll continue with a3 in order to prevent Black's pieces (mainly the knight) from accessing the b4-square.} 9.a3 9...Nd5 {Black jumps their knight to d5 and attacks the f4-pawn. But we'll play an inbetween move. We jump our knight to e4 and counterattack Black's bishop.} 10.Ne4 10...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to e7.} 11.Qd2 {We'll continue with Qd2, preparing to castle queenside and supporting the f-pawn. White now has a pleasant edge, and should look to continue developing with O-O-O, Nf3 and Bd3. A model game from this position is Saric - Zelcic, 2018. Here's the link to the game: https://www.365chess.com/game.php?gid=4153730} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Caro-Kann Part 2 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...c5 and 4...Nc6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/25e3f0cf-0b90-49b1-862f-f5c5cf625314/studies/606388b1-3bc2-4ed2-96d4-59df6d4e9f81/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black goes for the Caro-Kann. We'll place both pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn. We grab some space and close the center by pushing the pawn to e5. The Advance Variation.} 3.e5 3...c5 {Black strikes in the center with ...c5 just over 20% of the time, challenging our d4-pawn. They lose a tempo, but at the same time hope we play c3 so that they achieve a French-like structure without their bishop being blocked in. But the better move is to capture on c5!} 4.dxc5 4...Nc6 {Black opts for ...Nc6 in this study, attacking our e5-pawn. Expect to see this more than 65% of the time. Once we capture on c5 and accept the sacrifice, our goal is to be as annoying as possible and try to hold on to our pawns while developing. That's why we will defend the e5-pawn with f4!} 5.f4 5...e6 {Most often does Black try to go after our c5-pawn. We develop the dark-squared bishop to e3 and support the c5-pawn. ...Qa5+ doesn't really work because of c3 with b4 ideas.} 6.Be3 6...d4 {Things might seem dangerous if Black pushes the d-pawn to d4, attacking our bishop and interfering with the defense of the c5-pawn. We'll calmly slide our bishop to f2 and allow Black to take on c5.} 7.Bf2 7...Bxc5 {Black captures the c5-pawn. They have gotten their pawn back, but have extended the d4-pawn in the process and weakened the e4-square. We develop our knight to d2, eyeing e4 and preparing to castle queenside in the future.} 8.Nd2 8...Nge7 {Black develops the knight to e7, preparing ...O-O and looking to jump the knight to d5 or f5. Before anything else, we'll continue with a3 in order to prevent Black's pieces (mainly the knight) from accessing the b4-square.} 9.a3 9...Nf5 {Black jumps their knight into f5, aiming for the e3-square. We develop our bishop to d3. Ideally, we would like to set up with Qf3, Ne2 and O-O-O.} 10.Bd3 10...Ne3 {Black jumps the knight into e3 and attacks our queen, trying to disrupt our plans.} 11.Qf3 {But we can simply move the queen to f3. White now has a pleasant position and a strong objective edge. We can look to continue improving the knights and castle queenside. We should capture the knight on e3 at some point soon.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Caro-Kann Part 2 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...c5 and 4...Nc6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/25e3f0cf-0b90-49b1-862f-f5c5cf625314/studies/606388b1-3bc2-4ed2-96d4-59df6d4e9f81/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black goes for the Caro-Kann. We'll place both pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn. We grab some space and close the center by pushing the pawn to e5. The Advance Variation.} 3.e5 3...c5 {Black strikes in the center with ...c5 just over 20% of the time, challenging our d4-pawn. They lose a tempo, but at the same time hope we play c3 so that they achieve a French-like structure without their bishop being blocked in. But the better move is to capture on c5!} 4.dxc5 4...Nc6 {Black opts for ...Nc6 in this study, attacking our e5-pawn. Expect to see this more than 65% of the time. Once we capture on c5 and accept the sacrifice, our goal is to be as annoying as possible and try to hold on to our pawns while developing. That's why we will defend the e5-pawn with f4!} 5.f4 5...Nh6 {Black develops the kingside knight out to h6, supporting the g4-square and looking to jump the knight to f5 in the future. We'll respond with c3. This is a useful move which controls the important d4-square and supports the situational b4.} 6.c3 6...Bg4 {Black uses the control of the g4-square and activates the bishop with tempo on our queen. This is better than Black playing ...e6 and leaving the bishop stuck. The most natural defense is Be2, offering a trade.} 7.Be2 7...Bxe2 {Black exchanges bishops on e2. We recapture with the queen.} 8.Qxe2 8...e6 {Finally, Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and attacks our c5-pawn. We'll play another logical move, which is developing to e3 and defending. We still aren't letting Black getting any pawns back.} 9.Be3 9...Nf5 {Black jumps the knight into f5 and threatens our dark-squared bishop.} 10.Bf2 {We slide the bishop back to f2. White is still up a pawn and has a very comfortable position. Ideally, we would like to achieve Nf3, O-O and b4. If Black now pushes d4, we should respond by moving our queen to c4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Caro-Kann Part 2 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...c5 and 4...Nc6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/25e3f0cf-0b90-49b1-862f-f5c5cf625314/studies/606388b1-3bc2-4ed2-96d4-59df6d4e9f81/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black goes for the Caro-Kann. We'll place both pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn. We grab some space and close the center by pushing the pawn to e5. The Advance Variation.} 3.e5 3...c5 {Black strikes in the center with ...c5 just over 20% of the time, challenging our d4-pawn. They lose a tempo, but at the same time hope we play c3 so that they achieve a French-like structure without their bishop being blocked in. But the better move is to capture on c5!} 4.dxc5 4...Nc6 {Black opts for ...Nc6 in this study, attacking our e5-pawn. Expect to see this more than 65% of the time. Once we capture on c5 and accept the sacrifice, our goal is to be as annoying as possible and try to hold on to our pawns while developing. That's why we will defend the e5-pawn with f4!} 5.f4 5...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5. This doesn't really challenge our structure anymore. We develop our kingside knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...e6 {Black opens up an attack on the c5-pawn.} 7.Be3 {We'll defend with the bishop and claim an advantage. White can look to continue with Bb5 to pin the knight and O-O. If Black now tries to regain the pawn with ...Qa5+, we can simply push c3 and the c5-pawn will be untouchable due to b4 ideas.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Caro-Kann Part 2 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...e6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/25e3f0cf-0b90-49b1-862f-f5c5cf625314/studies/e3a6ca56-6ec3-4133-a862-766f55a983cd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black goes for the Caro.} 2.d4 {We expand in the center with d4.} 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. We'll respond with the Advance Variation.} 3.e5 3...c5 {Black strikes with ...c5, attacking our structure. We'll take.} 4.dxc5 4...e6 {Black plays ...e6 and opens up an attack on the c5-pawn a quarter of the time. We'll respond with a3! It looks strange to build from the flank early like this, but our idea is direct: b4.} 5.a3 5...Bxc5 {Black captures the c5-pawn. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3. If Black now tries to chip away at our center with ...f6, we should continue with b4 and Bb2.} 6.Nf3 6...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight. We should attack the bishop with b4 at some point anyway.} 7.b4 7...Bb6 {Black moves the bishop back to b6. Since we've expanded with tempo, we'll fianchetto now.} 8.Bb2 {We fianchetto the c1-bishop, and have now transposed to 6...Nc6.} 8...Nge7 {Black develops the kingside knight, preparing ...O-O and looking to jump the knight to g6. It's all about the e5-pawn. We develop our bishop to d3. The main move, 9...Ng6, now transposes to the main line.} 9.Bd3 9...O-O {Black castles their king, but this is very risky! We push the b-pawn and attack Black's knight! We dislodge the attacker of the e5-pawn first and lure one of Black's potential defenders away.} 10.b5 10...Na5 {Black jumps the knight out to a5. Why did we do this? Well, once Black castled, the h7-square immediately became vulnerable and open to the Greek Gift!} 11.Bxh7+ 11...Kxh7 {Black captures with the king. We'll give the standard check with the knight on g5. Of course, ...Kg8 or ...Kh8 is out of the question because it instantly loses to Qh5.} 12.Ng5+ 12...Kg6 {Black moves the king up to g6, which is the best attempt to survive. We'll bring our queen to g4 in that case and threaten a lot of nasty discoveries.} 13.Qg4 13...f5 {Black pushes ...f5 and attacks our queen, which is to be expected.} 14.exf6 {We capture en passant. Practically speaking, this is position is incredibly difficult to defend with Black. The engine recommends a line where Black sacrifices the bishop on f2 and then plays ...e5, but this is tough to find.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Caro-Kann Part 2 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...e6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/25e3f0cf-0b90-49b1-862f-f5c5cf625314/studies/e3a6ca56-6ec3-4133-a862-766f55a983cd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black goes for the Caro.} 2.d4 {We expand in the center with d4.} 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. We'll respond with the Advance Variation.} 3.e5 3...c5 {Black strikes with ...c5, attacking our structure. We'll take.} 4.dxc5 4...e6 {Black plays ...e6 and opens up an attack on the c5-pawn a quarter of the time. We'll respond with a3! It looks strange to build from the flank early like this, but our idea is direct: b4.} 5.a3 5...Nc6 {Black can also play 5...Nc6, but it will quickly transpose. We'll develop our knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Bxc5 {Black captures the c5-pawn. Once it arrives there, b4 is coming.} 7.b4 7...Bb6 {Black moves the bishop back to b6. Since we've expanded with tempo, we'll fianchetto now.} 8.Bb2 {We fianchetto the c1-bishop, and have now transposed to 6...Nc6.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Caro-Kann Part 2 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...e6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/25e3f0cf-0b90-49b1-862f-f5c5cf625314/studies/e3a6ca56-6ec3-4133-a862-766f55a983cd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black goes for the Caro.} 2.d4 {We expand in the center with d4.} 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. We'll respond with the Advance Variation.} 3.e5 3...c5 {Black strikes with ...c5, attacking our structure. We'll take.} 4.dxc5 4...e6 {Black plays ...e6 and opens up an attack on the c5-pawn a quarter of the time. We'll respond with a3! It looks strange to build from the flank early like this, but our idea is direct: b4.} 5.a3 5...Bxc5 {Black captures the c5-pawn. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3. If Black now tries to chip away at our center with ...f6, we should continue with b4 and Bb2.} 6.Nf3 6...Ne7 {Black develops the kingside knight, preparing ...O-O and looking to jump to g6. We'll develop our bishop to d3, eyeing the kingside. Black now has to be very careful!} 7.Bd3 7...Ng6 {Black proceeds with their plan and jumps the knight into g6. We'll castle our king, having completed kingside development. Black now has two options, and should choose carefully!} 8.O-O 8...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight, adding an attacker to the e5-pawn. This is the safer option.} 9.b4 {Still, we get to expand on the queenside and attack Black's bishop with b4. White now has a slight edge, and should look to continue developing with Bb2, Re1 and Nbd2.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Caro-Kann Part 2 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...e6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/25e3f0cf-0b90-49b1-862f-f5c5cf625314/studies/e3a6ca56-6ec3-4133-a862-766f55a983cd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black goes for the Caro.} 2.d4 {We expand in the center with d4.} 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. We'll respond with the Advance Variation.} 3.e5 3...c5 {Black strikes with ...c5, attacking our structure. We'll take.} 4.dxc5 4...e6 {Black plays ...e6 and opens up an attack on the c5-pawn a quarter of the time. We'll respond with a3! It looks strange to build from the flank early like this, but our idea is direct: b4.} 5.a3 5...Bxc5 {Black captures the c5-pawn. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3. If Black now tries to chip away at our center with ...f6, we should continue with b4 and Bb2.} 6.Nf3 6...Ne7 {Black develops the kingside knight, preparing ...O-O and looking to jump to g6. We'll develop our bishop to d3, eyeing the kingside. Black now has to be very careful!} 7.Bd3 7...Ng6 {Black proceeds with their plan and jumps the knight into g6. We'll castle our king, having completed kingside development. Black now has two options, and should choose carefully!} 8.O-O 8...O-O {Black castles their king. Surprisingly enough, this is very risky, even with the knight on g6! We kickstart an attack by jumping our knight into g5. This knight jump, in conjunction with Qh5, could cause Black serious problems.} 9.Ng5 9...Be7 {Black slides the bishop back to e7 and attacks our knight. We'll move our queen out to h5, supporting the knight and threatening checkmate on h7!} 10.Qh5 10...Bxg5 {Black captures the strong knight, which was their idea all along. We recapture with the bishop. If Black now moves the queen to b6, we can continue with Nd2-Nf3. Two model games are Bjerre - Olsen and Gaponenko - Belenkaya, 2019.} 11.Bxg5 11...Qc7 {Black escapes to c7 and attacks the e5-pawn instead.} 12.f4 {We support the e-pawn with f4. White is now better as we have more space, attacking prospects on the kingside and the bishop pair!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Caro-Kann Part 2 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...e6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/25e3f0cf-0b90-49b1-862f-f5c5cf625314/studies/e3a6ca56-6ec3-4133-a862-766f55a983cd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black goes for the Caro.} 2.d4 {We expand in the center with d4.} 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. We'll respond with the Advance Variation.} 3.e5 3...c5 {Black strikes with ...c5, attacking our structure. We'll take.} 4.dxc5 4...e6 {Black plays ...e6 and opens up an attack on the c5-pawn a quarter of the time. We'll respond with a3! It looks strange to build from the flank early like this, but our idea is direct: b4.} 5.a3 5...Bxc5 {Black captures the c5-pawn. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3. If Black now tries to chip away at our center with ...f6, we should continue with b4 and Bb2.} 6.Nf3 6...Ne7 {Black develops the kingside knight, preparing ...O-O and looking to jump to g6. We'll develop our bishop to d3, eyeing the kingside. Black now has to be very careful!} 7.Bd3 7...O-O {Black castles their king, but this natural move is a massive blunder! We get the Greek Gift sacrifice.} 8.Bxh7+ 8...Kxh7 {Black captures with the king. We'll follow up with Ng5+.} 9.Ng5+ 9...Kg6 {Black tries ...Kg6.} 10.h4 {We'll continue with h4, supporting the knight and preparing to push the pawn to h5. White is currently down a piece, but simply has a decisive attack and a winning advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Caro-Kann Part 2 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...e6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/25e3f0cf-0b90-49b1-862f-f5c5cf625314/studies/e3a6ca56-6ec3-4133-a862-766f55a983cd/lines"]

1.e4 1...c6 {Black goes for the Caro.} 2.d4 {We expand in the center with d4.} 2...d5 {Black plays ...d5. We'll respond with the Advance Variation.} 3.e5 3...c5 {Black strikes with ...c5, attacking our structure. We'll take.} 4.dxc5 4...e6 {Black plays ...e6 and opens up an attack on the c5-pawn a quarter of the time. We'll respond with a3! It looks strange to build from the flank early like this, but our idea is direct: b4.} 5.a3 5...Bxc5 {Black captures the c5-pawn. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3. If Black now tries to chip away at our center with ...f6, we should continue with b4 and Bb2.} 6.Nf3 6...Ne7 {Black develops the kingside knight, preparing ...O-O and looking to jump to g6. We'll develop our bishop to d3, eyeing the kingside. Black now has to be very careful!} 7.Bd3 7...Ng6 {Black proceeds with their plan and jumps the knight into g6. We'll castle our king, having completed kingside development. Black now has two options, and should choose carefully!} 8.O-O 8...O-O {Black castles their king. Surprisingly enough, this is very risky, even with the knight on g6! We kickstart an attack by jumping our knight into g5. This knight jump, in conjunction with Qh5, could cause Black serious problems.} 9.Ng5 9...h6 {Black attacks our knight with ...h6, but has forgotten about something. We can sacrifice the knight on f7! This simply removes the defender of the knight on g6.} 10.Nxf7 10...Rxf7 {Black recaptures with the rook. But the knight on g6 is hanging.} 11.Bxg6 11...Rf8 {Black retreats to f8.} 12.Qh5 {We move our queen out to h5. White now has a winning advantage as Black has blundered. We are simply up a pawn and have great attacking prospects on the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Pirc & Philidor Defenses (e4 d6) / Study 1: The Pirc Defense (3...g6 or 3...c6) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/f9b1f4ae-7c89-4f33-8c1d-e6dcf32e79a3/studies/50213cbf-831e-4744-9a2c-e563cffc0b45/lines"]

1.e4 1...d6 {In the Pirc, Black gives up the center 4% of the time and instead builds up slowly with ...d6, which happened in more than 135 million games in the database. Black either wants to play a quick fianchetto setup with ...g6 or strike in the center later with ...c6 and ...e5. Since allowed, we'll play d4.} 2.d4 2...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn. This is still a flexible move which allows Black to switch between multiple setups. We'll respond with a flexible developing move of our own.} 3.Nc3 3...c6 {Black plays ...c6 on move three, which is a sideline known as the Pribyl system, and it's played 13% of the time. Black will look to play ...Qa5 and ...e5 to challenge our center. We'll play h3, hoping to transpose into a more traditional Pirc. If Black now plays ...g6, we can set up with g4, Bg2 and Nge2.} 4.h3 4...Qa5 {Black moves the queen out to a5 and pins the c3-knight. The idea is direct: Black wants to capture our e4-pawn. We'll protect the e4-pawn by developing our bishop to d3.} 5.Bd3 5...e5 {Black expands in the center and creates tension between the pawns. They want us to capture on e5 so that they can open up the dark-squared bishop. Instead, we add support to the pawn by simply developing to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Nbd7 {Black develops the queenside knight. That leaves us room to castle since we've finished kingside development.} 7.O-O 7...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop to e7, preparing ...O-O. As you can see, this is a cramped but solid setup by Black. We'll add further support to our d4-pawn.} 8.Be3 8...O-O {Black castles their king.} 9.a4 {We'll continue with a4, expanding on the queenside and preventing any ...b5 ideas. White is now slightly better, and can look to continue with Qd2 and Re1.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Pirc & Philidor Defenses (e4 d6) / Study 1: The Pirc Defense (3...g6 or 3...c6) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/f9b1f4ae-7c89-4f33-8c1d-e6dcf32e79a3/studies/50213cbf-831e-4744-9a2c-e563cffc0b45/lines"]

1.e4 1...d6 {In the Pirc, Black gives up the center 4% of the time and instead builds up slowly with ...d6, which happened in more than 135 million games in the database. Black either wants to play a quick fianchetto setup with ...g6 or strike in the center later with ...c6 and ...e5. Since allowed, we'll play d4.} 2.d4 2...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn. This is still a flexible move which allows Black to switch between multiple setups. We'll respond with a flexible developing move of our own.} 3.Nc3 3...g6 {In the traditional Pirc, Black wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop since they've played ...d6. Expect to see this move more than 60% of the time. I recommend an aggressive approach against this, starting with h3. It may seem like a waiting move.} 4.h3 4...Bg7 {But once Black completes the fianchetto... We'll advance with g4! We want to surprise Black with extra space and suffocate their pieces as much as possible. We also leave room for our bishop to develop to g2.} 5.g4 5...O-O {Black castles their king. And we make use of the open g2-square.} 6.Bg2 6...Nc6 {Occasionally, Black doesn't strike in the center and instead develops the queenside knight to c6. In that case, we should be as aggressive as possible with d5. Grabbing this central space with tempo helps our position despite opening up Black's bishop.} 7.d5 7...Ne5 {Black jumps the knight into e5. But we'll continue chasing it with f4!} 8.f4 8...Ned7 {Black retreats the knight to d7.} 9.Nge2 {With the space advantage and potential attacking ideas on the kingside, White now has a pleasant edge. We can look to continue with Be3 and O-O. Our pawn structure is a bit overextended, but serves the crucial purpose of keeping Black's pieces passive.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Pirc & Philidor Defenses (e4 d6) / Study 1: The Pirc Defense (3...g6 or 3...c6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/f9b1f4ae-7c89-4f33-8c1d-e6dcf32e79a3/studies/50213cbf-831e-4744-9a2c-e563cffc0b45/lines"]

1.e4 1...d6 {In the Pirc, Black gives up the center 4% of the time and instead builds up slowly with ...d6, which happened in more than 135 million games in the database. Black either wants to play a quick fianchetto setup with ...g6 or strike in the center later with ...c6 and ...e5. Since allowed, we'll play d4.} 2.d4 2...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn. This is still a flexible move which allows Black to switch between multiple setups. We'll respond with a flexible developing move of our own.} 3.Nc3 3...g6 {In the traditional Pirc, Black wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop since they've played ...d6. Expect to see this move more than 60% of the time. I recommend an aggressive approach against this, starting with h3. It may seem like a waiting move.} 4.h3 4...Bg7 {But once Black completes the fianchetto... We'll advance with g4! We want to surprise Black with extra space and suffocate their pieces as much as possible. We also leave room for our bishop to develop to g2.} 5.g4 5...O-O {Black castles their king. And we make use of the open g2-square.} 6.Bg2 6...e5 {Black expands in the center and creates tension between the pawns. We develop our knight to e2: that is the standard development square for the kingside knight in these structures. We prefer it if Black releases the tension, so we will try to keep it for as long as possible.} 7.Nge2 7...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight. We add further support to the central pawn by developing our bishop to e3. If Black continues playing slowly with a move like ...Re8, we can push d5.} 8.Be3 8...exd4 {Black releases the tension by capturing the d4-pawn instead. We recapture with the knight.} 9.Nxd4 9...Re8 {Black improves the rook. We'll hide our king.} 10.O-O 10...Bd7 {Black develops the bishop to d7.} 11.Nde2 {Black doesn't have much control over the center, and we'll go Ng3 and f4 and advance on the kingside. The position is still quite complex, but White has an edge. We should look to meet any ...Ne5 with b3, in order to prevent ...Nc4.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Pirc & Philidor Defenses (e4 d6) / Study 1: The Pirc Defense (3...g6 or 3...c6) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/f9b1f4ae-7c89-4f33-8c1d-e6dcf32e79a3/studies/50213cbf-831e-4744-9a2c-e563cffc0b45/lines"]

1.e4 1...d6 {In the Pirc, Black gives up the center 4% of the time and instead builds up slowly with ...d6, which happened in more than 135 million games in the database. Black either wants to play a quick fianchetto setup with ...g6 or strike in the center later with ...c6 and ...e5. Since allowed, we'll play d4.} 2.d4 2...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn. This is still a flexible move which allows Black to switch between multiple setups. We'll respond with a flexible developing move of our own.} 3.Nc3 3...g6 {In the traditional Pirc, Black wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop since they've played ...d6. Expect to see this move more than 60% of the time. I recommend an aggressive approach against this, starting with h3. It may seem like a waiting move.} 4.h3 4...Bg7 {But once Black completes the fianchetto... We'll advance with g4! We want to surprise Black with extra space and suffocate their pieces as much as possible. We also leave room for our bishop to develop to g2.} 5.g4 5...O-O {Black castles their king. And we make use of the open g2-square.} 6.Bg2 6...e5 {Black expands in the center and creates tension between the pawns. We develop our knight to e2: that is the standard development square for the kingside knight in these structures. We prefer it if Black releases the tension, so we will try to keep it for as long as possible.} 7.Nge2 7...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight. We add further support to the central pawn by developing our bishop to e3. If Black continues playing slowly with a move like ...Re8, we can push d5.} 8.Be3 8...exd4 {Black releases the tension by capturing the d4-pawn instead. We recapture with the knight.} 9.Nxd4 9...Nxd4 {Black trades the knights on d4. We recapture with the bishop.} 10.Bxd4 10...Re8 {Black places the rook on the semi-open e-file. We can simply castle out of the way.} 11.O-O 11...Bd7 {Black develops the bishop to d7. They are giving us a lot of space which we will utilize immediately. We'll expand with f4!} 12.f4 12...Bc6 {Black moves the bishop to c6 and attacks the e4-pawn. They will mostly try to target this pawn with their setups.} 13.Qd3 {We defend the e4-pawn by moving our queen up to d3. The a-rook will join the party next on either d1 or e1, and we can play a4, keeping the e5-idea in mind as well. It's important to know when to grab some space, like we did with f4, and when to play positionally and defend.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Pirc & Philidor Defenses (e4 d6) / Study 1: The Pirc Defense (3...g6 or 3...c6) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/f9b1f4ae-7c89-4f33-8c1d-e6dcf32e79a3/studies/50213cbf-831e-4744-9a2c-e563cffc0b45/lines"]

1.e4 1...d6 {In the Pirc, Black gives up the center 4% of the time and instead builds up slowly with ...d6, which happened in more than 135 million games in the database. Black either wants to play a quick fianchetto setup with ...g6 or strike in the center later with ...c6 and ...e5. Since allowed, we'll play d4.} 2.d4 2...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn. This is still a flexible move which allows Black to switch between multiple setups. We'll respond with a flexible developing move of our own.} 3.Nc3 3...g6 {In the traditional Pirc, Black wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop since they've played ...d6. Expect to see this move more than 60% of the time. I recommend an aggressive approach against this, starting with h3. It may seem like a waiting move.} 4.h3 4...Bg7 {But once Black completes the fianchetto... We'll advance with g4! We want to surprise Black with extra space and suffocate their pieces as much as possible. We also leave room for our bishop to develop to g2.} 5.g4 5...O-O {Black castles their king. And we make use of the open g2-square.} 6.Bg2 6...e5 {Black expands in the center and creates tension between the pawns. We develop our knight to e2: that is the standard development square for the kingside knight in these structures. We prefer it if Black releases the tension, so we will try to keep it for as long as possible.} 7.Nge2 7...exd4 {Black immediately releases the tension by capturing the d4-pawn. We recapture with the knight. The main move, 8...Nc6, now transposes to the main line after Be3.} 8.Nxd4 8...Re8 {Black moves the rook and puts it on the semi-open e-file. We simply castle our king as our center is defended.} 9.O-O 9...Nbd7 {Black develops the knight to d7. A lot of Black's ideas include the knight rotating to b6 or them trying to expand on the queenside. That's why a4 is a useful flank push.} 10.a4 10...Nc5 {Black jumps the knight into c5 instead and attacks the e4-pawn.} 11.Re1 {But we'll simply defend it with the rook. We should play around our central pawn and the space we have with moves like Bg5, Qd2 and a5, and a timely e5-push. We have a comfortable position and a lot of activity. As long as we keep our center intact, we will be fine.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Pirc & Philidor Defenses (e4 d6) / Study 1: The Pirc Defense (3...g6 or 3...c6) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/f9b1f4ae-7c89-4f33-8c1d-e6dcf32e79a3/studies/50213cbf-831e-4744-9a2c-e563cffc0b45/lines"]

1.e4 1...d6 {In the Pirc, Black gives up the center 4% of the time and instead builds up slowly with ...d6, which happened in more than 135 million games in the database. Black either wants to play a quick fianchetto setup with ...g6 or strike in the center later with ...c6 and ...e5. Since allowed, we'll play d4.} 2.d4 2...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn. This is still a flexible move which allows Black to switch between multiple setups. We'll respond with a flexible developing move of our own.} 3.Nc3 3...g6 {In the traditional Pirc, Black wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop since they've played ...d6. Expect to see this move more than 60% of the time. I recommend an aggressive approach against this, starting with h3. It may seem like a waiting move.} 4.h3 4...Bg7 {But once Black completes the fianchetto... We'll advance with g4! We want to surprise Black with extra space and suffocate their pieces as much as possible. We also leave room for our bishop to develop to g2.} 5.g4 5...O-O {Black castles their king. And we make use of the open g2-square.} 6.Bg2 6...c5 {Black strikes in the center with ...c5, which is pretty much as popular as the main move, ...e5. We'll proceed by capturing on c5, as there is no good way to defend the pawn. ...Qa5 is not dangerous now. We can simply respond by capturing on d6.} 7.dxc5 7...dxc5 {Black recaptures on c5. It's fine to trade the queens here. We have a concrete follow-up.} 8.Qxd8 8...Rxd8 {Black recaptures on d8. Our idea was to push in the center and attack the knight. The d5-square is unavailable.} 9.e5 9...Nfd7 {Black jumps to d7, though, counterattacking our pawn on e5.} 10.f4 {But we can defend it with f4. We should look to continue with Be3 and Nge2 and enjoy our slight advantage. Castling is flexible. We can go kingside or queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Pirc & Philidor Defenses (e4 d6) / Study 2: The Philidor Defense (3...e5) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/f9b1f4ae-7c89-4f33-8c1d-e6dcf32e79a3/studies/bd44240e-6a9a-44f2-baa2-86302ab5dc1e/lines"]

1.e4 1...d6 {Black gives up the center, and instead builds up slowly with ...d6. We'll expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...Nf6 {Black goes for the flexible ...Nf6. We'll defend the pawn.} 3.Nc3 3...e5 {The Philidor Defense is only played 6% of the time, but that's still more than 1.3 million games. Black challenges the center and is fine with us capturing on e5 and then trading the queens. But we'll support the pawn instead with Nge2, as we don't want to simplify too early.} 4.Nge2 4...Nbd7 {Black develops the knight to d7 around a quarter of the time. We'll go for our plan with h3.} 5.h3 5...c6 {One of Black's ideas in the Philidor is ...c6 to serve as a support for the ...b5 advancement. We'll play g4 anyway, preparing to activate our bishop.} 6.g4 6...Be7 {Before proceeding with their queenside plans, Black activates the bishop. We'll develop ours too.} 7.Bg2 7...O-O {Black castles their king. So will we.} 8.O-O 8...b5 {Finally, Black lashes out with ...b5. We are fine with this as it weakens their c6-pawn. Their whole structure is now shaky.} 9.a3 {Plus, it's easy to prevent ...b4 with a3. White now has a slight edge; We'll bring our knight to g6, and an expansion with f4 is possible. You can check out the model game from this position, Mirzoev - Yarmysty, 2018.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Pirc & Philidor Defenses (e4 d6) / Study 2: The Philidor Defense (3...e5) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/f9b1f4ae-7c89-4f33-8c1d-e6dcf32e79a3/studies/bd44240e-6a9a-44f2-baa2-86302ab5dc1e/lines"]

1.e4 1...d6 {Black gives up the center, and instead builds up slowly with ...d6. We'll expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...Nf6 {Black goes for the flexible ...Nf6. We'll defend the pawn.} 3.Nc3 3...e5 {The Philidor Defense is only played 6% of the time, but that's still more than 1.3 million games. Black challenges the center and is fine with us capturing on e5 and then trading the queens. But we'll support the pawn instead with Nge2, as we don't want to simplify too early.} 4.Nge2 4...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop to e7, preparing ...O-O. This occurs more than a quarter of the time. I recommend a similar approach to our last study with h3 and g4 against the Philidor as well.} 5.h3 5...O-O {Black castles their king. And we'll continue with g4.} 6.g4 6...exd4 {Black releases the tension by capturing the d4-pawn, the only line with independent value. If we recapture with the knight, Black will have ...d5, which is something we don't want to allow. So, we'll recapture with the queen!} 7.Qxd4 7...Nc6 {The downside of this choice is that Black can develop with tempo on our queen. So, we'll move it back to d2.} 8.Qd2 8...Be6 {Black develops the bishop to e6. That's the most active square they have on their disposal.} 9.Bg2 {And we'll finalize our kingside development. White now has a slight edge, and we will look to proceed with f4 and O-O. We've successfully prevented ...d5 and restricted Black's pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/0048b730-d295-4d1e-87ab-0b02ab7c812f/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {The Modern Defense is similar to the Pirc in the sense of fianchettoing the bishop. Expect to see it only 3% of the time, but it occured in more than 125 million games. By choosing this move order, Black is flexible with their pawn pushes and doesn't always have to commit to ...d6. We'll play d4 anyway.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight. There are various pawn pushes Black can now implement, such as ...c6, ...c5 or ...d6.} 3.Nf3 3...d6 {The most common reply is 3...d6, which is played more than 40% of the time. Black opens up the bishop and prepares to fight against our center in hypermodern fashion. We'll develop our bishop to c4, and our idea is to play Qe2, castle, and wait for the right moment to go e5.} 4.Bc4 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn 45% of the time. We defend the e-pawn and support a future e5 by moving our queen to e2.} 5.Qe2 5...O-O {Black castles their king. This is the most common move by a big margin. Still, Black can't really prevent our e5 advancement.} 6.e5 6...dxe5 {Black captures the pawn on e5. We'll recapture with the pawn.} 7.dxe5 7...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll play the standard h3 idea.} 8.h3 8...Nb6 {Black could move the knight back to b6 and threaten our bishop. We'll simply slide it back to b3.} 9.Bb3 9...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight. Let's castle out of the way.} 10.O-O 10...Nd4 {Black chooses an aggressive route and offers a knight trade on d4. We will gladly accept.} 11.Nxd4 11...Qxd4 {Black recaptures on d4 and attacks the e5-pawn.} 12.Re1 {We support the e5-pawn by moving our rook to the e-file. We have a slight advantage and can look to continue with Nd2 and Nf3. Black's knight on b6 is dominated by our bishop and the piece we should look to target right now is the queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/0048b730-d295-4d1e-87ab-0b02ab7c812f/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {The Modern Defense is similar to the Pirc in the sense of fianchettoing the bishop. Expect to see it only 3% of the time, but it occured in more than 125 million games. By choosing this move order, Black is flexible with their pawn pushes and doesn't always have to commit to ...d6. We'll play d4 anyway.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight. There are various pawn pushes Black can now implement, such as ...c6, ...c5 or ...d6.} 3.Nf3 3...d6 {The most common reply is 3...d6, which is played more than 40% of the time. Black opens up the bishop and prepares to fight against our center in hypermodern fashion. We'll develop our bishop to c4, and our idea is to play Qe2, castle, and wait for the right moment to go e5.} 4.Bc4 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn 45% of the time. We defend the e-pawn and support a future e5 by moving our queen to e2.} 5.Qe2 5...O-O {Black castles their king. This is the most common move by a big margin. Still, Black can't really prevent our e5 advancement.} 6.e5 6...dxe5 {Black captures the pawn on e5. We'll recapture with the pawn.} 7.dxe5 7...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll play the standard h3 idea.} 8.h3 8...c6 {Black supports the knight with ...c6. We simply castle our king. If Black now expands with ...b5, we should capture on d5 and move our rook to the d-file.} 9.O-O 9...Nd7 {Black develops the queenside knight. They want to jump to b6. We'll move our rook to the e-file, overprotecting the e5-pawn.} 10.Re1 10...N7b6 {Black jumps the knight into b6 and threatens our bishop.} 11.Bb3 {We slide the bishop back to b3. White has a great position and can continue developing with Nbd2. We should meet ...a5 with a4.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/0048b730-d295-4d1e-87ab-0b02ab7c812f/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {The Modern Defense is similar to the Pirc in the sense of fianchettoing the bishop. Expect to see it only 3% of the time, but it occured in more than 125 million games. By choosing this move order, Black is flexible with their pawn pushes and doesn't always have to commit to ...d6. We'll play d4 anyway.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight. There are various pawn pushes Black can now implement, such as ...c6, ...c5 or ...d6.} 3.Nf3 3...d6 {The most common reply is 3...d6, which is played more than 40% of the time. Black opens up the bishop and prepares to fight against our center in hypermodern fashion. We'll develop our bishop to c4, and our idea is to play Qe2, castle, and wait for the right moment to go e5.} 4.Bc4 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn 45% of the time. We defend the e-pawn and support a future e5 by moving our queen to e2.} 5.Qe2 5...O-O {Black castles their king. This is the most common move by a big margin. Still, Black can't really prevent our e5 advancement.} 6.e5 6...Ne8 {Black retreats the knight to e8. We've pushed the knight back. Let's restrict the light-squared bishop further with h3 now.} 7.h3 7...c5 {Black tries to undermine our structure by striking in the center with ...c5. Not much merit to letting Black trade off many pawns in the center, so we'll capture instead.} 8.dxc5 8...dxe5 {Black captures the e5-pawn, which they deem more useful than the c5-pawn. That's okay. We'll just castle. We shouldn't rush with recapturing on e5, as it could become dangerous if our king is not safe.} 9.O-O 9...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight.} 10.c3 {We'll continue with c3, preventing Black's knight from jumping to d4. The position is relatively balanced, and White can continue with Re1 or Rd1. A model game from this position is Hansen - Hoerstmann, 2017. Here's the link to the game: https://lichess.org/yon0zdzC/white} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/0048b730-d295-4d1e-87ab-0b02ab7c812f/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {The Modern Defense is similar to the Pirc in the sense of fianchettoing the bishop. Expect to see it only 3% of the time, but it occured in more than 125 million games. By choosing this move order, Black is flexible with their pawn pushes and doesn't always have to commit to ...d6. We'll play d4 anyway.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight. There are various pawn pushes Black can now implement, such as ...c6, ...c5 or ...d6.} 3.Nf3 3...d6 {The most common reply is 3...d6, which is played more than 40% of the time. Black opens up the bishop and prepares to fight against our center in hypermodern fashion. We'll develop our bishop to c4, and our idea is to play Qe2, castle, and wait for the right moment to go e5.} 4.Bc4 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn 45% of the time. We defend the e-pawn and support a future e5 by moving our queen to e2.} 5.Qe2 5...O-O {Black castles their king. This is the most common move by a big margin. Still, Black can't really prevent our e5 advancement.} 6.e5 6...dxe5 {Black captures the pawn on e5. We'll recapture with the pawn.} 7.dxe5 7...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll play the standard h3 idea.} 8.h3 8...c6 {Black supports the knight with ...c6. We simply castle our king. If Black now expands with ...b5, we should capture on d5 and move our rook to the d-file.} 9.O-O 9...Be6 {Black develops the bishop to e6. And we'll activate our bishop to d2. The main idea behind this move is to support the knight coming to c3.} 10.Bd2 10...Nd7 {Black develops the queenside knight.} 11.Nc3 {We'll continue with Nc3 and reach a relatively balanced position. White will continue with Rfe1 and Rad1 and proceed to maneuver with the knights to e4 and d4 to pressure Black's position. We should keep an eye on our e5-pawn at all times, because it's the thorn in Black's position. That's why we are building behind it.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/0048b730-d295-4d1e-87ab-0b02ab7c812f/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {The Modern Defense is similar to the Pirc in the sense of fianchettoing the bishop. Expect to see it only 3% of the time, but it occured in more than 125 million games. By choosing this move order, Black is flexible with their pawn pushes and doesn't always have to commit to ...d6. We'll play d4 anyway.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight. There are various pawn pushes Black can now implement, such as ...c6, ...c5 or ...d6.} 3.Nf3 3...d6 {The most common reply is 3...d6, which is played more than 40% of the time. Black opens up the bishop and prepares to fight against our center in hypermodern fashion. We'll develop our bishop to c4, and our idea is to play Qe2, castle, and wait for the right moment to go e5.} 4.Bc4 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn 45% of the time. We defend the e-pawn and support a future e5 by moving our queen to e2.} 5.Qe2 5...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, negating the effect of the c4-bishop and looking to support a future central expansion. We can push e5, though, before Black gets any chance to expand.} 6.e5 6...dxe5 {Black captures the pawn on e5. We'll recapture with the pawn and keep the pressure on the knight.} 7.dxe5 7...Nd5 {Black escapes to the center. Since the knight has left its post, there is some weakness on the dark squares. We can look to take advantage of this by bringing our bishop to g5 and pressuring Black's queen.} 8.Bg5 8...Qd7 {Black moves the queen to the only safe square, d7. Let's try to trade off Black's active knight from the center. If Black now attempts to support their knight with ...c6, we should expose the d6-outpost with Ne4 and Nd6.} 9.Nc3 9...Nxc3 {Black trades the knights on c3 instead. We'll recapture.} 10.bxc3 10...O-O {Black castles their king.} 11.O-O {And so do we. White is now simply better. We do have the inferior pawn structure, but we control more space and our pieces are incredibly active. We can continue with Rad1 and Rfe1, and h4 is always on the cards.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/0048b730-d295-4d1e-87ab-0b02ab7c812f/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {The Modern Defense is similar to the Pirc in the sense of fianchettoing the bishop. Expect to see it only 3% of the time, but it occured in more than 125 million games. By choosing this move order, Black is flexible with their pawn pushes and doesn't always have to commit to ...d6. We'll play d4 anyway.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight. There are various pawn pushes Black can now implement, such as ...c6, ...c5 or ...d6.} 3.Nf3 3...d6 {The most common reply is 3...d6, which is played more than 40% of the time. Black opens up the bishop and prepares to fight against our center in hypermodern fashion. We'll develop our bishop to c4, and our idea is to play Qe2, castle, and wait for the right moment to go e5.} 4.Bc4 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn 45% of the time. We defend the e-pawn and support a future e5 by moving our queen to e2.} 5.Qe2 5...c6 {Black continues with ...c6, looking to support a future expansion in the center and on the queenside. This gives us time to push our e-pawn forward and attack Black's knight! The move 6...dxe5 would now transpose to the main line.} 6.e5 6...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. It's important to prevent the bishop from coming to g4, so we'll play h3 for now.} 7.h3 7...O-O {Black castles their king.} 8.O-O {The position is still in the balance, and White can look to continue with Re1 or Rd1 and a4 to play against ...b5. If Black attacks our bishop with ...b5 right now, we should capture on d5 and proceed with improving the rook and developing the bishop to f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 1: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/0048b730-d295-4d1e-87ab-0b02ab7c812f/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {The Modern Defense is similar to the Pirc in the sense of fianchettoing the bishop. Expect to see it only 3% of the time, but it occured in more than 125 million games. By choosing this move order, Black is flexible with their pawn pushes and doesn't always have to commit to ...d6. We'll play d4 anyway.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight. There are various pawn pushes Black can now implement, such as ...c6, ...c5 or ...d6.} 3.Nf3 3...d6 {The most common reply is 3...d6, which is played more than 40% of the time. Black opens up the bishop and prepares to fight against our center in hypermodern fashion. We'll develop our bishop to c4, and our idea is to play Qe2, castle, and wait for the right moment to go e5.} 4.Bc4 4...e6 {Black decides to restrict our bishop with ...e6 12% of the time. However, this approach is passive and leaves their own bishop stuck on c8. We'll castle for now.} 5.O-O 5...Ne7 {Black develops the knight to e7. We'll continue improving and bring the rook to the e-file this time.} 6.Re1 6...O-O {Black castles their king.} 7.c3 {We'll continue with c3, negating the effect of Black's g7-bishop and supporting the d4-pawn. White now has a slight edge and can continue with the simple Bf4 and Nbd2.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...c6 or 3...c5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/e1d733fc-721e-4c6b-9984-1c3b5d9372aa/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {Black is intending to fianchetto their bishop. We'll expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight.} 3.Nf3 3...c5 {Black plays ...c5, trying to activate the g7-bishop, 7% of the time. This isn't particularly good at all. We can actually call Black's bluff and capture on c5.} 4.dxc5 4...Qa5+ {The idea is to get the queen out to a5 and fork our king and the pawn. We'll block with c3.} 5.c3 5...Qxc5 {Black captures the pawn on c5. We'll play the creative Na3, aiming for Nb5 and Be3. This move is the antidote for Black's approach.} 6.Na3 6...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and threatens the e4-pawn. It's most logical to push the pawn to e5.} 7.e5 7...Ng4 {Black jumps to g4. It seems like we can't save the e5-pawn, since it's attacked with three pieces. But we counted on this and we have Qd4, offering a trade!} 8.Qd4 8...d6 {Black opens up the c8-bishop and supports the queen with ...d6. We'll trade the queens. We have a plan B.} 9.Qxc5 9...dxc5 {Black recaptures on c5. Instead of defending the e5-pawn, we can jump our knight to b5 and threaten the devastating Nc7.} 10.Nb5 10...Na6 {Black covers the c7-square, but their knight is passive.} 11.Bf4 {We finally support the e5-pawn by developing the dark-squared bishop. Black's pieces are all over the place and waiting to be attacked, while we have harmony in the position. White has a borderline winning advantage and should continue with h3, Be2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...c6 or 3...c5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/e1d733fc-721e-4c6b-9984-1c3b5d9372aa/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {Black is intending to fianchetto their bishop. We'll expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight.} 3.Nf3 3...c6 {Black continues with ...c6, preparing a central expansion with ...d5, just under 5% of the time. This reminds of the Caro-Kann, except the dark-squared bishop is already on g7. We'll get both knights out.} 4.Nc3 4...d5 {Black goes for ...d5. They would love to activate their light-squared bishop next, which is why we will play h3.} 5.h3 5...dxe4 {Black captures the pawn on e4. We'll recapture.} 6.Nxe4 6...Nf6 {Most often, Black develops the kingside knight to f6. We'll take.} 7.Nxf6+ 7...exf6 {Black recaptures with the e-pawn. This doubles their pawns but keeps their kingside safer. We'll bring our bishop to c4 next.} 8.Bc4 8...O-O {Black castles their king. We'll do the same.} 9.O-O 9...Nd7 {Black develops the queenside knight. We'll improve our rook. Both sides developing calmly and in a traditional manner.} 10.Re1 10...Nb6 {Black jumps the knight into b6 and attacks the c4-bishop.} 11.Bb3 {But we can simply slide the bishop back to b3. White now has a pleasant edge, and can look to continue with a4 and Bf4.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...c6 or 3...c5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/e1d733fc-721e-4c6b-9984-1c3b5d9372aa/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {Black is intending to fianchetto their bishop. We'll expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight.} 3.Nf3 3...c6 {Black continues with ...c6, preparing a central expansion with ...d5, just under 5% of the time. This reminds of the Caro-Kann, except the dark-squared bishop is already on g7. We'll get both knights out.} 4.Nc3 4...d5 {Black goes for ...d5. They would love to activate their light-squared bishop next, which is why we will play h3.} 5.h3 5...dxe4 {Black captures the pawn on e4. We'll recapture.} 6.Nxe4 6...Nd7 {Black develops the queenside knight. We'll prepare kingside castling by developing the bishop to the most active square.} 7.Bc4 7...Ngf6 {Black develops the kingside knight. It's more efficient to capture than to defend our knight or retreat.} 8.Nxf6+ 8...Nxf6 {Black recaptures with the d7-knight. We'll castle.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {Black castles their king too.} 10.Re1 {We'll move our rook to the e-file. White now has a pleasant position and can look to continue with c3 and Bf4, strengthening the presence on the dark squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...c6 or 3...c5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/e1d733fc-721e-4c6b-9984-1c3b5d9372aa/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {Black is intending to fianchetto their bishop. We'll expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight.} 3.Nf3 3...c6 {Black continues with ...c6, preparing a central expansion with ...d5, just under 5% of the time. This reminds of the Caro-Kann, except the dark-squared bishop is already on g7. We'll get both knights out.} 4.Nc3 4...d5 {Black goes for ...d5. They would love to activate their light-squared bishop next, which is why we will play h3.} 5.h3 5...Nf6 {If Black activates the knight before capturing on e4, we shouldn't waste any time. Let's push e5. If Black now retreats the knight, we can continue with Be3 and Qd2.} 6.e5 6...Ne4 {Black jumps the knight into e4 instead. We'll trade. There's no reason to leave Black's knight in the center.} 7.Nxe4 7...dxe4 {Black recaptures on e4. We'll attack the weak e4-pawn by jumping our knight to g5.} 8.Ng5 8...c5 {Black pushes the c-pawn and counterattacks our center. A move like c3 is definitely playable here, but I recommend we capture on c5. It's a more direct approach that asks Black whether they want to trade queens.} 9.dxc5 9...Qa5+ {Looking to immediately regain the pawn, Black moves the queen out to a5 and gives a check to our king. This leads to a fun line where we eventually provoke Black to triple their pawns on the e-file! We'll block the check with c3, for starters.} 10.c3 10...Qxc5 {Black captures the pawn on c5. The e5-pawn is vulnerable. However, if Black didn't want to trade queens on d1, let's see if they want to do it on d4.} 11.Qd4 11...Qxe5 {Nope. Black captures on e5. But we didn't just place our queen on d4 so that we trade. We'll slide to c4 now and attack both the bishop and the f7-pawn. If Black now castles their king, we have a nice tactic with Nxf7!} 12.Qc4 12...Be6 {Black deals with both threats by moving the bishop to e6. We'll trade.} 13.Nxe6 13...Qxe6 {Black recaptures with the queen. We trade queens on e6, forcing Black to recapture and triple their e-pawns! This whole line is pretty much forced.} 14.Qxe6 14...fxe6 {Black recaptures on e6.} 15.Be3 {We'll develop our bishop to e3 and White is simply better. Black's tripled and isolated pawns on the e-file yield more than enough compensation for the pawn that we are down. We can look to continue by long castling.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...c6 or 3...c5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/e1d733fc-721e-4c6b-9984-1c3b5d9372aa/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {Black is intending to fianchetto their bishop. We'll expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight.} 3.Nf3 3...c6 {Black continues with ...c6, preparing a central expansion with ...d5, just under 5% of the time. This reminds of the Caro-Kann, except the dark-squared bishop is already on g7. We'll get both knights out.} 4.Nc3 4...d5 {Black goes for ...d5. They would love to activate their light-squared bishop next, which is why we will play h3.} 5.h3 5...Nf6 {If Black activates the knight before capturing on e4, we shouldn't waste any time. Let's push e5. If Black now retreats the knight, we can continue with Be3 and Qd2.} 6.e5 6...Ne4 {Black jumps the knight into e4 instead. We'll trade. There's no reason to leave Black's knight in the center.} 7.Nxe4 7...dxe4 {Black recaptures on e4. We'll attack the weak e4-pawn by jumping our knight to g5.} 8.Ng5 8...c5 {Black pushes the c-pawn and counterattacks our center. A move like c3 is definitely playable here, but I recommend we capture on c5. It's a more direct approach that asks Black whether they want to trade queens.} 9.dxc5 9...Qxd1+ {Black trades the queens on d1. We'll recapture.} 10.Kxd1 10...Bxe5 {Black captures the e5-pawn. And we see a trade of pawns in the center.} 11.Nxe4 11...Bf5 {Black develops the light-squared bishop with tempo.} 12.Bd3 {We support our knight with Bd3. White is now simply up a pawn and can continue with c3 and Kc2. Black's ...c5 attempt hasn't proven worthy at all.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...c6 or 3...c5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/e1d733fc-721e-4c6b-9984-1c3b5d9372aa/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {Black is intending to fianchetto their bishop. We'll expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight.} 3.Nf3 3...c5 {Black plays ...c5, trying to activate the g7-bishop, 7% of the time. This isn't particularly good at all. We can actually call Black's bluff and capture on c5.} 4.dxc5 4...Qa5+ {The idea is to get the queen out to a5 and fork our king and the pawn. We'll block with c3.} 5.c3 5...Qxc5 {Black captures the pawn on c5. We'll play the creative Na3, aiming for Nb5 and Be3. This move is the antidote for Black's approach.} 6.Na3 6...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and threatens the e4-pawn. It's most logical to push the pawn to e5.} 7.e5 7...Ng4 {Black jumps to g4. It seems like we can't save the e5-pawn, since it's attacked with three pieces. But we counted on this and we have Qd4, offering a trade!} 8.Qd4 8...Qxd4 {Black trades the queens on d4.} 9.cxd4 {We recapture with the c-pawn and now our center is back together. White now has a considerable edge. We want to play Nb5 and h3 to force the knight back. From there, we will target Black's weaknesses.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...c6 or 3...c5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/e1d733fc-721e-4c6b-9984-1c3b5d9372aa/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {Black is intending to fianchetto their bishop. We'll expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight.} 3.Nf3 3...c5 {Black plays ...c5, trying to activate the g7-bishop, 7% of the time. This isn't particularly good at all. We can actually call Black's bluff and capture on c5.} 4.dxc5 4...Qa5+ {The idea is to get the queen out to a5 and fork our king and the pawn. We'll block with c3.} 5.c3 5...Qxc5 {Black captures the pawn on c5. We'll play the creative Na3, aiming for Nb5 and Be3. This move is the antidote for Black's approach.} 6.Na3 6...d6 {Black opens up their light-squared bishop with ...d6. We develop our bishop to e3 and attack Black's queen! If Black now retreats the queen to c7, we can continue with Nb5.} 7.Be3 7...Qa5 {Black moves the queen to a5, allowing an interesting idea. We can sacrifice the pawn on e5! We want to either trade off Black's bishop or make them block it.} 8.e5 8...Bxe5 {Black captures the pawn with their bishop. We'll take.} 9.Nxe5 9...dxe5 {Black recaptures with the d-pawn. Otherwise there would have been Bd4 problems.} 10.Bc4 {We are down a pawn, but the piece activity and the lead in development more than compensate for it. We can continue with Qb3 and castle. Meanwhile, Black has only got their queen developed, and their e5-pawn is a liability more than it is an asset.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Modern Defense (e4 g6) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...c6 or 3...c5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/ebf1eca3-3db9-4118-b791-a2ac82e9227e/studies/e1d733fc-721e-4c6b-9984-1c3b5d9372aa/lines"]

1.e4 1...g6 {Black is intending to fianchetto their bishop. We'll expand in the center with d4.} 2.d4 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop the kingside knight.} 3.Nf3 3...c6 {Black continues with ...c6, preparing a central expansion with ...d5, just under 5% of the time. This reminds of the Caro-Kann, except the dark-squared bishop is already on g7. We'll get both knights out.} 4.Nc3 4...d5 {Black goes for ...d5. They would love to activate their light-squared bishop next, which is why we will play h3.} 5.h3 5...dxe4 {Black captures the pawn on e4. We'll recapture.} 6.Nxe4 6...Nf6 {Most often, Black develops the kingside knight to f6. We'll take.} 7.Nxf6+ 7...Bxf6 {Black recaptures with the bishop. Let's finish kingside development.} 8.Bc4 8...O-O {Black castles, which allows for a positional idea by us. We move our bishop out to h6 and attack Black's rook, since Black has left a hole on g7. If Black now defends with ...Bg7, we should respond with Qd2 and O-O.} 9.Bh6 9...Re8 {Black moves the rook to e8 instead.} 10.O-O {We castle our king. We should look to consolidate and improve with c3, Re1 and Qd2. We have a lead in activity and there is no real coordination between Black's pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Black's Other Move 1 Options / Study 1: The Rest (1...b6/Nf6/Nc6) / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/5b4fd4ba-c4e4-4f26-b469-f2daef1c302e/studies/bcff9c8d-0805-4721-b3d1-37408549279f/lines"]

1.e4 1...Nc6 {Black replies with 1...Nc6, which is only played 1% of the time. With this move, Black hopes that you will play d4, get met with ...d5, and an immediate fight in the center. I recommend we proceed with 2. Nc3. The move ...e5 now transposes to the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, supporting a future advance with ...d5. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3.} 3.Nf3 3...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We'll expand in the center with d4, as we don't want to trade and open up Black's pieces. Our general plan is now Be3 and castling.} 4.d4 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and adds an attacker to the e4-pawn. We defend the e-pawn by developing our light-squared bishop to d3. If Black now goes for ...Nb4, we should continue with e5 and, ideally, Bg5.} 5.Bd3 5...Bb4 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and pins our c3-knight. We'll simply castle. We have a plan if Black goes after our knight.} 6.O-O 6...Bxc3 {Black does capture. We have to throw in exd5, because we lose the e4-pawn otherwise.} 7.exd5 7...Nxd4 {Black captures on d4 with the knight. We'll recapture on c3, naturally.} 8.bxc3 8...Nxf3+ {Black captures the knight on f3. We'll recapture with the queen.} 9.Qxf3 9...Qxd5 {Black captures on d5 with the queen.} 10.Qg3 {And we slide our queen over to g3 and attack two of Black's pawns. The bishop pair, in conjunction with the superior piece activity, should secure White a pleasant game. Even though we've got doubled c-pawns and are down a pawn, we have a lot of compensation.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Black's Other Move 1 Options / Study 1: The Rest (1...b6/Nf6/Nc6) / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/5b4fd4ba-c4e4-4f26-b469-f2daef1c302e/studies/bcff9c8d-0805-4721-b3d1-37408549279f/lines"]

1.e4 1...Nc6 {Black replies with 1...Nc6, which is only played 1% of the time. With this move, Black hopes that you will play d4, get met with ...d5, and an immediate fight in the center. I recommend we proceed with 2. Nc3. The move ...e5 now transposes to the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, supporting a future advance with ...d5. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3.} 3.Nf3 3...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We'll expand in the center with d4, as we don't want to trade and open up Black's pieces. Our general plan is now Be3 and castling.} 4.d4 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and adds an attacker to the e4-pawn. We defend the e-pawn by developing our light-squared bishop to d3. If Black now goes for ...Nb4, we should continue with e5 and, ideally, Bg5.} 5.Bd3 5...Bb4 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and pins our c3-knight. We'll simply castle. We have a plan if Black goes after our knight.} 6.O-O 6...Bxc3 {Black does capture. We have to throw in exd5, because we lose the e4-pawn otherwise.} 7.exd5 7...Bxd4 {Black captures on d4 with the bishop. In that case, we'll take the knight.} 8.dxc6 8...Bb6 {Black retreats the bishop to b6 to avoid the trade.} 9.Ne5 {And we jump our knight to e5. White now has a comfortable advantage and can continue with Re1, Bg5 and Qf3. The idea is to put pressure on the f6-knight and the kingside overall since we are ahead in development and activity.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Black's Other Move 1 Options / Study 1: The Rest (1...b6/Nf6/Nc6) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/5b4fd4ba-c4e4-4f26-b469-f2daef1c302e/studies/bcff9c8d-0805-4721-b3d1-37408549279f/lines"]

1.e4 1...Nc6 {Black replies with 1...Nc6, which is only played 1% of the time. With this move, Black hopes that you will play d4, get met with ...d5, and an immediate fight in the center. I recommend we proceed with 2. Nc3. The move ...e5 now transposes to the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, supporting a future advance with ...d5. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3.} 3.Nf3 3...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move by advancing the d-pawn and challenging our central pawn. We'll expand in the center with d4, as we don't want to trade and open up Black's pieces. Our general plan is now Be3 and castling.} 4.d4 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and adds an attacker to the e4-pawn. We defend the e-pawn by developing our light-squared bishop to d3. If Black now goes for ...Nb4, we should continue with e5 and, ideally, Bg5.} 5.Bd3 5...Bb4 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and pins our c3-knight. We'll simply castle. We have a plan if Black goes after our knight.} 6.O-O 6...Bxc3 {Black does capture. We have to throw in exd5, because we lose the e4-pawn otherwise.} 7.exd5 7...Nxd5 {Black captures on d5 with the knight. We'll take the bishop.} 8.bxc3 8...Nxc3 {Black recaptures on c3, attacking our queen. The d2-square is natural to attack the knight.} 9.Qd2 9...Nd5 {Black moves the knight back to d5.} 10.c4 {We take some space and attack Black's knight once more with c4! Down a pawn, but our piece activity and lead in development more than compensate for it. Black has lost a lot of tempi as well. We'll look to continue with Bb2, Rad1 and Rfe1.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Black's Other Move 1 Options / Study 1: The Rest (1...b6/Nf6/Nc6) / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/5b4fd4ba-c4e4-4f26-b469-f2daef1c302e/studies/bcff9c8d-0805-4721-b3d1-37408549279f/lines"]

1.e4 1...Nc6 {Black replies with 1...Nc6, which is only played 1% of the time. With this move, Black hopes that you will play d4, get met with ...d5, and an immediate fight in the center. I recommend we proceed with 2. Nc3. The move ...e5 now transposes to the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...d6 {Black continues with ...d6, opening up the light-squared bishop.} 3.d4 {We expand in the center with d4. Of course, White has an objective edge, and can look to set up with Nge2, h3 and g4, taking a note from Chapter 5.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Black's Other Move 1 Options / Study 1: The Rest (1...b6/Nf6/Nc6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/5b4fd4ba-c4e4-4f26-b469-f2daef1c302e/studies/bcff9c8d-0805-4721-b3d1-37408549279f/lines"]

1.e4 1...b6 {Black goes for the Owen Defense 2% of the time, and it happened in almost 80 million games in the database. Black wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and focus their play around our e4-pawn. We'll place both pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop and attacks the e4-pawn. I recommend defending with the bishop here instead of committing with Nc3. We should leave our options open for the c-pawn to move.} 3.Bd3 3...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop and preparing to fight against our center. We'll develop the kingside knight to f3 for now.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {Black immediately strikes with ...c5. Now it's logical to connect the pawns with c3.} 5.c3 5...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn. We can protect the pawn with the queen. This is better than pushing e5, since we would just be giving the d5-square to Black. And we aren't looking to push c4 anymore once we've played c3.} 6.Qe2 6...d5 {However, once Black expands with ...d5, that square is taken by the pawn. So we will push e5 now! This closes the center with tempo and opens up our light-squared bishop.} 7.e5 7...Nfd7 {Black moves the knight back to d7. I suggest we jump forward here. Black shouldn't play ...h6 because we can sacrifice on e6 and expose their weakened light squares.} 8.Ng5 8...Be7 {They attack our knight with the bishop instead.} 9.f4 {But we placed the knight on g5 to keep it there, so we will protect it with f4. White now simply has a pleasant position with promising attacking prospects in the middlegame. We'll look to continue with Be3 and O-O. Black might have a hard time castling.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Black's Other Move 1 Options / Study 1: The Rest (1...b6/Nf6/Nc6) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/5b4fd4ba-c4e4-4f26-b469-f2daef1c302e/studies/bcff9c8d-0805-4721-b3d1-37408549279f/lines"]

1.e4 1...b6 {Black goes for the Owen Defense 2% of the time, and it happened in almost 80 million games in the database. Black wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and focus their play around our e4-pawn. We'll place both pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop and attacks the e4-pawn. I recommend defending with the bishop here instead of committing with Nc3. We should leave our options open for the c-pawn to move.} 3.Bd3 3...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop and preparing to fight against our center. We'll develop the kingside knight to f3 for now.} 4.Nf3 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e4-pawn. We'll push to e5 and counterattack. I prefer this aggressive option to defending the pawn.} 5.e5 5...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. This is where we see why leaving room for the c-pawn is important. We take some space and attack Black's knight once more with c4!} 6.c4 6...Nb4 {Black jumps the knight into b4 and threatens to capture our bishop.} 7.Be2 {But we simply slide back to e2. White should look to continue with a3, Nc3 and O-O. Black might have a hard time fighting against our active central pawn structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Black's Other Move 1 Options / Study 1: The Rest (1...b6/Nf6/Nc6) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/5b4fd4ba-c4e4-4f26-b469-f2daef1c302e/studies/bcff9c8d-0805-4721-b3d1-37408549279f/lines"]

1.e4 1...b6 {Black goes for the Owen Defense 2% of the time, and it happened in almost 80 million games in the database. Black wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and focus their play around our e4-pawn. We'll place both pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop and attacks the e4-pawn. I recommend defending with the bishop here instead of committing with Nc3. We should leave our options open for the c-pawn to move.} 3.Bd3 3...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop and preparing to fight against our center. We'll develop the kingside knight to f3 for now.} 4.Nf3 4...d5 {Black quickly expands with ...d5. Both e5 and capturing the pawn are playable, but I recommend taking so that we can win an additional tempo. Black will rarely ever recapture with the pawn because that condemns the bishop on b7 to death.} 5.exd5 5...Bxd5 {Black recaptures with the bishop. We'll attack it with c4.} 6.c4 6...Bb7 {Black retreats the queen to d8 instead.} 7.Nc3 {And we develop our queenside knight. The position is quite pleasant and we have relatively simple development with Qe2, Bf4 and castling. We want to take even more control of the e5-square and of the e-file in general, utilizing our space advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Black's Other Move 1 Options / Study 1: The Rest (1...b6/Nf6/Nc6) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/5b4fd4ba-c4e4-4f26-b469-f2daef1c302e/studies/bcff9c8d-0805-4721-b3d1-37408549279f/lines"]

1.e4 1...b6 {Black goes for the Owen Defense 2% of the time, and it happened in almost 80 million games in the database. Black wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and focus their play around our e4-pawn. We'll place both pawns in the center.} 2.d4 2...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop and attacks the e4-pawn. I recommend defending with the bishop here instead of committing with Nc3. We should leave our options open for the c-pawn to move.} 3.Bd3 3...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop and preparing to fight against our center. We'll develop the kingside knight to f3 for now.} 4.Nf3 4...d5 {Black quickly expands with ...d5. Both e5 and capturing the pawn are playable, but I recommend taking so that we can win an additional tempo. Black will rarely ever recapture with the pawn because that condemns the bishop on b7 to death.} 5.exd5 5...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. We expand in the center and attack Black's queen with c4. If Black now moves the queen to h5, we should defend with Be2 and then proceed with Nc3 and O-O.} 6.c4 6...Qd8 {Black retreats the queen to d8 instead.} 7.Nc3 {And we develop our queenside knight. The position is quite pleasant and we have relatively simple development with Qe2, Bf4 and castling. We want to take even more control of the e5-square and of the e-file in general, utilizing our space advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Black's Other Move 1 Options / Study 1: The Rest (1...b6/Nf6/Nc6) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/5b4fd4ba-c4e4-4f26-b469-f2daef1c302e/studies/bcff9c8d-0805-4721-b3d1-37408549279f/lines"]

1.e4 1...Nf6 {Black replies with 1...Nf6 2% of the time, which signifies the Alekhine's Defense. The main idea is to allow White to advance the pawns in the center with tempo and hopefully overextend, before striking back at some point. We should still push e5 and attack.} 2.e5 2...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. And we'll continue chasing the knight. In moderate amounts, pushing these pawns grabs healthy space with tempo without risking the overextension.} 3.c4 3...Nb6 {Black moves the knight back to b6. And a4 threatens to trap the knight.} 4.a4 4...a5 {Black stops a5 directly, but allows for an extremely useful idea. We lift our rook up to a3! Inspired by Jeroen Bosch's article in SOS/New in Chess, I'm sharing this idea with you. If Black now goes for ...d6, we should capture and slide our rook over to g3!} 5.Ra3 5...e6 {Black pushes ...e6 and opens up an attack on our rook.} 6.Rg3 {We move our rook over to g3! Our flexible rook is now an additional annoying piece on the kingside, and hard to attack. The arising position is roughly equal, but White has strong attacking chances. The move ...d5 should now be met with cxd5.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Black's Other Move 1 Options / Study 1: The Rest (1...b6/Nf6/Nc6) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/5b4fd4ba-c4e4-4f26-b469-f2daef1c302e/studies/bcff9c8d-0805-4721-b3d1-37408549279f/lines"]

1.e4 1...Nf6 {Black replies with 1...Nf6 2% of the time, which signifies the Alekhine's Defense. The main idea is to allow White to advance the pawns in the center with tempo and hopefully overextend, before striking back at some point. We should still push e5 and attack.} 2.e5 2...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. And we'll continue chasing the knight. In moderate amounts, pushing these pawns grabs healthy space with tempo without risking the overextension.} 3.c4 3...Nb6 {Black moves the knight back to b6. And a4 threatens to trap the knight.} 4.a4 4...d5 {Black pushes the pawn up to d5. This move frees up the d7-square and takes some space in the center. In order to avoid the trade of pawns and helping Black improve their position, we'll push and attack the knight.} 5.c5 5...N6d7 {Black retreats the knight to d7 and threatens the c- and e-pawns. But d4 protects both.} 6.d4 6...b6 {Expect Black to chip away at our structure in the early game in the Alekhine. This does force us to push the b-pawn as well and defend. Our structure is extended and shaky, but that's fine.} 7.b4 7...a5 {Black is persistent and continues with ...a5 as well. Instead of trying to defend, let's implement an interesting tactic. We sacrifice the e-pawn with e6! The idea of this move is to weaken Black's light squares and to make it difficult for Black to develop the rest of their pieces.} 8.e6 8...fxe6 {Black captures the pawn on e6.} 9.Nf3 {We develop our knight to f3, and are looking to jump it into g5, with a slight advantage. We could have played e6 earlier but pushing it on move 8 makes sure Black has already committed to attacking our queenside. Now their pawn structure is the one that's shaky, and the e-pawns don't help matters.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Black's Other Move 1 Options / Study 1: The Rest (1...b6/Nf6/Nc6) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/1c85b58c-c773-42a3-ac19-b4e4afff3aa2/chapters/5b4fd4ba-c4e4-4f26-b469-f2daef1c302e/studies/bcff9c8d-0805-4721-b3d1-37408549279f/lines"]

1.e4 1...Nf6 {Black replies with 1...Nf6 2% of the time, which signifies the Alekhine's Defense. The main idea is to allow White to advance the pawns in the center with tempo and hopefully overextend, before striking back at some point. We should still push e5 and attack.} 2.e5 2...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. And we'll continue chasing the knight. In moderate amounts, pushing these pawns grabs healthy space with tempo without risking the overextension.} 3.c4 3...Nb6 {Black moves the knight back to b6. And a4 threatens to trap the knight.} 4.a4 4...d6 {Black goes for ...d6, challenging our e5-pawn and opening up the d7-square for the knight. We'll still attack it with a5, though.} 5.a5 5...N6d7 {Black retreats the knight to the only safe square, d7. Our idea was to disallow the Black queen to recapture on d6. Now we'll take.} 6.exd6 6...cxd6 {Black recaptures with the c-pawn.} 7.d4 {And we expand in the center with d4. With the space advantage in the center and on the queenside, White already has a slight advantage. We can look to continue by developing both knights, and then go for Be2 and O-O.} *